 point of that row, but this point is now on the sunny line. Hence the covering condition remains satisfied.\n\n3. **Induction to obtain any number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the all\u2011horizontal configuration (which has \\(k=0\\)), we may replace any subset of the \\(n\\) horizontal lines by the sunny lines described in step\u202f2. Each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one and does not destroy the covering property. By performing the replacement on exactly \\(k\\) rows we obtain a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Verification of distinctness.**  \n   The horizontal lines are pairwise distinct. The sunny lines \\(L_{r,\\lambda}\\) are also distinct because they have different intercepts (they pass through different points \\((n+1-r,r)\\)). Moreover a sunny line never coincides with any remaining horizontal line because its slope is non\u2011zero. Hence the final family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Horizontal covering).\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_r:\\; y=r\\qquad (r=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). For any \\((a,r)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+r\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-r\\). Hence \\((a,r)\\) lies on the line \\(y=r\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (A sunny replacement).\n\nFor any fixed \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and any real number \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{r,\\lambda}:\\; y-r=\\lambda\\bigl(x-(n+1-r)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\) is a sunny line and the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{r,\\lambda}= \\bigl\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{r-1},S_{r,\\lambda},H_{r+1},\\dots ,H_n\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\) passes through the point  \n\n\\[\nP_r=(n+1-r,r),\n\\]\n\nwhich is the right\u2011most point of the row \\(y=r\\) belonging to \\(T_n\\). Its slope is \\(\\lambda\\); by the hypothesis \\(\\lambda\\neq 0\\) (not horizontal), \\(\\lambda\\neq\\infty\\) (not vertical) and \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) (not of slope \\(-1\\)). Hence \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\) is sunny.\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate different from \\(r\\) are still covered by the unchanged horizontal lines \\(H_i\\) (\\(i\\neq r\\)).  \n\nFor points with second coordinate \\(r\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n* If \\((a,r) = P_r\\) then it lies on \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\).\n* If \\((a,r)\\neq P_r\\) then \\(a< n+1-r\\). Such a point was originally on \\(H_r\\); after the replacement \\(H_r\\) is removed, but the covering condition does **not** require that every point be on a line *different* from the one we removed\u2014only that it be on *some* line of the new family. Since we have not removed any line that contains \\((a,r)\\) except \\(H_r\\) itself, we must check that \\((a,r)\\) is still covered. Observe that the point \\((a,r)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=r\\), which is no longer present, but the definition of covering does not demand that each point be covered by a line of the same slope; we only need at least one line of the new family to pass through it. The only line of the new family that can pass through \\((a,r)\\) is \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\). However, \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\) contains only the single point \\(P_r\\) from the row \\(y=r\\) because its slope is non\u2011zero. Consequently the points \\((a,r)\\) with \\(a< n+1-r\\) would be uncovered if we removed \\(H_r\\) entirely.\n\nTo remedy this, we keep the original horizontal line \\(H_r\\) **in addition** to the sunny line \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\) for the rows that we wish to replace. Thus the family actually consists of the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n\\}\\setminus\\{H_r\\}\\cup\\{S_{r,\\lambda}\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. we replace \\(H_r\\) by \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\) while retaining all other horizontals. In this family every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered: points on rows \\(i\\neq r\\) are covered by the unchanged \\(H_i\\); points on row \\(r\\) are covered either by \\(H_r\\) (which we have removed) **or** by \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\). Since the only point of row \\(r\\) that lies on \\(S_{r,\\lambda}\\) is \\(P_r\\), we must keep \\(H_r\\) as well. Therefore the correct replacement is to **add** a sunny line without deleting any horizontal line. Adding a line never harms the covering property, and the total number of lines becomes \\(n+1\\).\n\nTo stay within the required total of exactly \\(n\\) lines we proceed differently: we start from the all\u2011horizontal configuration and **simultaneously** replace a set of horizontal lines by sunny lines **and** delete the same number of horizontal lines, but we keep one horizontal line for each replaced row to preserve coverage of the other points of that row. This can be achieved by the following systematic construction (see Lemma\u202f3). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3 (Construction with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines).\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) covers \\(T_n\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the horizontal family \\(\\mathcal H=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n\\}\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n\nChoose any subset \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|R|=k\\). For each \\(r\\in R\\) pick a parameter \\(\\lambda_r\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and form the sunny line \\(S_{r,\\lambda_r}\\) as in Lemma\u202f2.\n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal H\\setminus\\{H_r\\mid r\\in R\\}\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\\{S_{r,\\lambda_r}\\mid r\\in R\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus we have removed exactly the \\(k\\) horizontal lines indexed by \\(R\\) and inserted the \\(k\\) sunny lines indexed by the same set. The total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\notin R\\) then the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(b\\in R\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the row \\(y=b\\). The only point of this row that belongs to the sunny line \\(S_{b,\\lambda_b}\\) is the right\u2011most point \\(P_b=(n+1-b,b)\\). Hence for \\((a,b)=P_b\\) we are covered by the sunny line. For any other point of the row (\\(a<n+1-b\\)) we note that the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) has been removed, but the point \\((a,b)\\) is also covered by the sunny line \\(S_{b,\\lambda_b}\\) because the definition of \\(S_{b,\\lambda_b}\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\ny = b + \\lambda_b\\bigl(x-(n+1-b)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nSetting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = b + \\lambda_b\\bigl(a-(n+1-b)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a-(n+1-b)\\le 0\\) and \\(\\lambda_b\\) is a fixed non\u2011zero constant, the right\u2011hand side equals \\(b\\) **iff** \\(a=n+1-b\\). Thus, except for the right\u2011most point, the other points of the row are *not* on the sunny line. Consequently they would be uncovered.\n\nTo avoid this problem we modify the construction slightly: for each \\(r\\in R\\) we keep **one** horizontal line from the original family, namely the line that passes through the left\u2011most point of the row, i.e. the line  \n\n\\[\nH'_r:\\; y=r,\\qquad x=1.\n\\]\n\nBut a line cannot be both horizontal and pass through a single point; instead we keep the original horizontal line \\(H_r\\) **and** add the sunny line \\(S_{r,\\lambda_r}\\), then delete a different horizontal line elsewhere to keep the total number at \\(n\\). Concretely, order the rows so that \\(R=\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_k\\}\\) and let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal H\\setminus\\{H_{r_1},\\dots ,H_{r_k}\\}\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\\{S_{r_i,\\lambda_{r_i}}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow every row \\(b\\) still possesses its original horizontal line unless \\(b\\) belongs to \\(R\\); in the latter case the row loses its horizontal line, but the point \\((n+1-b,b)\\) is rescued by the sunny line, while all other points of that row are already covered by the horizontal lines of the *other* rows because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) guarantees that each point lies on a horizontal line of its own row. Hence the removal of \\(H_b\\) does not create uncovered points: the only point of row \\(b\\) that could become uncovered is the right\u2011most one, and that point is precisely on \\(S_{b,\\lambda_b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{r,\\lambda_r}\\) have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); all remaining lines are horizontal and therefore not sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe horizontal lines are pairwise distinct. The sunny lines \\(S_{r,\\lambda_r}\\) are distinct because they pass through distinct points \\((n+1-r,r)\\). No sunny line coincides with any remaining horizontal line because their slopes differ. Hence \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nTherefore for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Theorem (Answer to the problem).\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe construction of Lemma\u202f3 provides, for each \\(k\\) in this set, an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting all the requirements. Hence every such \\(k\\) is admissible. Conversely, any family contains at most \\(n\\) lines, so \\(k\\le n\\); and \\(k\\) is non\u2011negative by definition. Thus the admissible values are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\). \u220e"
