ecause any other lattice point would have to satisfy a linear equation whose only integer solutions are the two already listed.\n\n   By taking the first \\(k\\) of these lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n4. **Choosing the remaining non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   U_k=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\subseteq S_n\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of points already covered by the sunny lines.  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\setminus U_k\\) are still covered by the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).  \n\n   Because each family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the union \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) consists of \\(3n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   From these we select **any** \\(n-k\\) lines that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\setminus U_k\\).  \n   This is always possible: start with the whole family \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\); delete any line that contains only points already in \\(U_k\\); continue deleting until exactly \\(n-k\\) lines remain.  \n   Since at most \\(k\\) lines can become redundant (each redundant line can contain at most the two points of a single \\(\\ell_i\\)), we never delete more than \\(2k\\) lines, and because the original collection has \\(3n\\) lines we certainly can finish with \\(n-k\\) lines.  \n\n   The selected \\(n-k\\) lines are pairwise distinct (they belong to different members of the three families) and are non\u2011sunny by construction.\n\n5. **Verification of the whole configuration.**  \n\n   *The number of lines.* We have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) and exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, for a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n\n   *Sunny vs. non\u2011sunny.* A sunny line has slope \\(-2\\); every non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence no line belongs to both