0)\\) (which lies outside the set \\(T_{n}\\)).  \n   Because the slopes \\(\\frac{1}{s-1}\\) are distinct for distinct \\(s\\), the lines \\(\\ell_{s}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Remaining non\u2011sunny horizontal lines.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus S .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(r\\in R\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   h_{r}:\\; y=r .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) and are distinct from each other and from \\(L_{h}\\).\n\n   Altogether we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(L_{v})+1\\;(L_{h})+k\\;(\\ell_{s})+(n-2-k)\\;(h_{r})=n\n   \\]\n\n   lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n3. **Verification that every required point is covered.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).\n\n   *If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(L_{h}\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(L_{v}\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\)*, write \\(b=r\\).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(r\\in R\\) then the horizontal line \\(h_{r}\\) contains \\((a,r)\\).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(r\\in S\\) (so \\(r=s\\) for some \\(s\\in S\\)), then \\((a,r)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \n     Indeed, substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) with \\(s=r\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     y=\\frac{1}{r-1}a+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{r-1}\\Bigr)\n       =\\frac{a-(r-1)}{r-1}+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\((a,r)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-r\\le r-1\\) (the last inequality follows from \\(r\\ge2\\) and \\(a+r\\le n+1\\)).  \n     Hence \\(a-(r-1)\\le0\\) and the right\u2011hand side equals \\(r\\).  Thus \\((a,r)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_{r}\\) and belongs to that line.\n\n   In every case \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the constructed lines.  Therefore the family covers the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n   From step\u202f1 we know that any admissible family must contain the tw