- For every \\(s\\in\\mathcal{S}\\) **remove** \\(\\ell_{s}\\) and **add** \\(\\sigma_{s}\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y})\\;+\\;(n-2-|\\mathcal{S}|)\\;(\\text{kept }\\ell_{s})\\;+\\;|\\mathcal{S}|\\;(\\sigma_{s})=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the \\(k=|\\mathcal{S}|\\) lines \\(\\sigma_{s}\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification of coverage\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(\\ell_{x}\\) or \\(\\ell_{y}\\) respectively.\n\n*If \\(a,b\\ge 2\\)*, set \\(s=a+b\\) (so \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n- If \\(s\\notin\\mathcal{S}\\) the point lies on the retained line \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \n- If \\(s\\in\\mathcal{S}\\) then the point may be different from \\(P_{s}\\). In that case we use the following observation: among the points with sum \\(s\\) and both coordinates at least \\(2\\) the distinguished point \\(P_{s}\\) is the **only** one that lies on the sunny line \\(\\sigma_{s}\\) (because \\(\\sigma_{s}\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\)). All the remaining points with sum \\(s\\) are still covered by the axis lines: indeed for any such point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b=s\\) we have either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) after subtracting \\(1\\) from both coordinates, i.e. the point \\((a-1,b-1)\\) lies on either \\(\\ell_{x}\\) or \\(\\ell_{y}\\). Adding back the vector \\((1,1)\\) shows that the original point is on the line obtained by translating \\(\\ell_{x}\\) or \\(\\ell_{y}\\) by \\((1,1)\\); but such a translation is precisely the line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) which we have removed. However, because we removed \\(\\ell_{s}\\) only for the sums belonging to \\(\\mathcal{S}\\), the points with those sums are **still** covered by the sunny line \\(\\sigma_{s}\\) when they coincide with \\(P_{s}\\) and by the axis lines when one of the coordinates equals \\(1\\). The only points of sum \\(s\\) that have both coordinates \\(\\ge 2\\) and are not \\(P_{s}\\) are therefore covered by the axis 