            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe line through any two of them is respectively \\(x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\)), \\(y=1\\) (slope \\(0\\)) or \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points.  \nSince the three points are distinct, at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, giving\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sharper restriction\n\nFor an integer \\(s\\ge 4\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s,\\ a,b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(D_s\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y=-x+s,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\) and contains two distinct points of \\(D_s\\), those two points would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), a contradiction.  \nThus a non‑\\(-1\\) line meets \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.\n\nFor \\(s=4\\) we have \\(|D_4|=1\\) (the single point \\((2,2)\\)).  \nFor every \\(s\\ge 5\\) we have \\(|D_s|\\ge 2\\). Consequently, to cover all points of \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 5\\) we must use a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n\nThere are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-5+1 = n-3\n\\]\n\nsuch diagonals (for \\(s=5,6,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \nHence any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-3\\) distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\), together with the two axis lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nThus the total number of non‑sunny lines is at least \\( (n-3)+2 = n-1\\), and therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{x}: x=1\\) (vertical, slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* \\(\\ell_{y}: y=1\\) (horizontal, slope \\(0\\));\n* For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{s}: y=-x+s .\n  \\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) contains exactly the points of \\(D_s\\).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{x}\\);  \n* if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{y}\\);  \n* otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(s=a+b\\) satisfies \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{s}\\).\n\nThus every required point lies on at least one of the lines, and all lines are non‑sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the two axis lines \\(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y}\\) and the diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{s}\\) for \\(5\\le s\\le n+1\\) (these are \\(n-3\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nReplace the line \\(\\ell_{4}\\) (which contains only the point \\((2,2)\\)) by the sunny line\n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\sigma\\) has slope \\(1\\) (neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)), passes through \\((2,2)\\), and is distinct from all other lines.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y})\\;+\\;(n-3)\\;(\\ell_{s},\\;5\\le s\\le n+1)\\;+\\;1\\;(\\sigma)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly one line (\\(\\sigma\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nAll points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) are covered by \\(\\ell_{x}\\) or \\(\\ell_{y}\\).  \nFor a point with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and sum \\(s\\ge 5\\) the argument of Section 3 applies, using the retained line \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \nThe remaining point \\((2,2)\\) (the only point of \\(D_4\\)) lies on \\(\\sigma\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Non‑existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with at least two sunny lines existed.  \nFrom the discussion in Section 2, each diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 5\\) must be covered by a line of slope \\(-1\\); these are \\(n-3\\) distinct lines.  \nTogether with the two axis lines this already uses \\(n-1\\) lines, leaving at most one line free for a sunny line.  \nThus at most one sunny line can appear, contradicting the assumption.  \nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le 1\\) and the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) show that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
