aining exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) supplied by Lemma 1.\nIt consists of the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(V_i\\), the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines\n\\(H_j\\) and the diagonal line \\(D\\); none is sunny.\n\nWe shall replace, one at a time, up to \\(k\\) of the vertical lines by sunny\nlines, keeping the covering property.\n\nAssume that after \\(t\\) replacements (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) we have a family\n\\(\\mathcal L_t\\) that\n\n* contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines,\n* still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nChoose a vertical line \\(V_c\\) that has not yet been replaced\n(\\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\)).\nSelect a slope \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=\\alpha(x-c)+1\n\\]\n\nbe the line of slope \\(\\alpha\\) passing through the point \\((c,1)\\).\nBecause \\(\\alpha\\) avoids the three forbidden values, \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\nForm \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) by deleting \\(V_c\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) and inserting\n\\(\\ell\\).\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* The point \\((c,1)\\) is now on \\(\\ell\\) by construction.\n* Any point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-c\\) lies on the horizontal line\n  \\(H_b\\) (still present) or on the diagonal line \\(D\\) when \\(b=n-c\\); hence it\n  remains covered after the removal of \\(V_c\\).\n* All other points of \\(T_n\\) do not belong to \\(V_c\\) and therefore stay on the\n  same line as before (either a remaining vertical line, a horizontal line, or\n  \\(D\\)).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) satisfies the required properties and contains\n\\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nRepeating the replacement procedure \\(k\\) times yields a family\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 3.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line; write it as \\(y=mx+b\\) with\n\\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite.\nIf \\((a,b_1)\\) and \\((a,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\) are two points of \\(T_n\\) on\n\\(\\ell\\), then \\(a\\) is fixed and the two points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.\nBecause the slope is not infinite, \\(\\ell\\) can intersect a given vertical line\nin at most one point; hence at most one point of \\(T_n\\) can have a given\n\\(x\\)-coordinate.  \n\nSimilarly, because the slope is not \\(0\\), at most one point of \\(T_n\\) can have\na given \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \n\nConsequently any three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) would have to possess three\ndifferent \\(x\\)-coordinates and three different \\(y\\)-coordinates, which would\nforce the three points to be non‑collinear (the slope would be forced to be\n\\(-1\\) or \\(0\\)).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\);\nthe maximum is two. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Non‑existence of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma 3 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\nUsing (1),\n\n\\[\n2k\\le 2n < \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}=|T_n|\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nThus the sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\); the remaining\n\\(n-k\\le1\\) non‑sunny lines would have to cover the uncovered points.\nA non‑sunny line belongs to \\(\\mathcal V\\), \\(\\mathcal H\\) or \\(\\mathcal D\\).\nEach such line meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in at most \\(n-2\\) lattice\npoints (the vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the set in the points\n\\((c,1),\\dots,(c,n-c)\\), i.e. \\(n-c\\le n-2\\); the same bound holds for horizontal\nlines, and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) points, but it\nis already present in the construction of Lemma 1 and cannot be used to\ncompensate the deficit created by the sunny lines).  \nHence the at most one non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n-2\\) points, far\nshort of the \\(|T_n|-2k\\) points that remain uncovered.  This contradiction\nshows that no configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) exists.\n\nTherefore the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\n* For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists\n  (Lemma 2).  \n* No configuration exists for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) (Lemma 5).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] ∎"
