\\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus a **non\u2011sunny** line has one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h(c): y=c,\\qquad \n\\ell_v(c): x=c,\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}(c): x+y=c,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Covering by \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_n=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,y=j\\;\\big|\\;j=2,3,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,x+y=n\\,\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) consists of  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)+1+1=n-2\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, all of the types (1).  \n\n*Proof that \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal N_n}L\\supseteq S_n\\).*  \n\nWrite \\(S_n\\) as the disjoint union of the rows  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b=j\\},\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(2\\le j\\le n-2\\) the whole row \\(R_j\\) is contained in the horizontal line \\(y=j\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal N_n\\).  \n\nThe bottom row \\(R_1=\\{(1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\\) is contained in the line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal N_n\\).  \n\nThe single point \\((1,n-1)\\) (the leftmost point of the top row) satisfies \\(x+y=n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\in\\mathcal N_n\\).  \n\nAll remaining points of the top row have \\(b=n-1\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\); each such point also satisfies \\(x+y=n\\) and is thus covered.  \n\nHence every element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N_n\\). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Realising any \\(k\\le n-2\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n2. For each \\(\\alpha_i\\) pick a real number \\(\\beta_i\\) that is **not** of the form \\(b-\\alpha_i a\\) for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n   Since for a fixed \\(\\alpha_i\\) the set \\(\\{\\,b-\\alpha_i a\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\,\\}\\) is finite, such a \\(\\beta_i\\) exists.  \n   Define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\alpha_i x+\\beta_i .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(L_i\\cap S_n=\\varnothing\\).\n\n3. Start from the covering family \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) of Lemma\u202f1 (which uses exactly \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines).  \n   Replace arbitrarily \\(k\\) of its lines by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines still cover all points of \\(S_n\\) because the removed lines were redundant: every point of \\(S_n\\) already lies on one of the lines that remain (the proof of Lemma\u202f1 shows that each point is covered by a *specific* line, and we never delete that specific line).  \n\n4. If after the replacement we have fewer than \\(n\\) lines, add further distinct non\u2011sunny lines of the forms (1) that do not coincide with any line already present (for instance, vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots\\) or horizontal lines \\(y=n-1\\)).  \n\nThe resulting collection \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\), and still covers every point of \\(S_n\\). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition (Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.* Immediate from Lemma\u202f2. \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case 1: \\(k=n\\).*  \nAll lines are sunny.  \nConsider the three families  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nC=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach family lies on a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) respectively, i.e. on a non\u2011sunny direction.  \nA sunny line can intersect each of these families in **at most one** point, because two distinct points of the same family have the same difference of coordinates, which would force the line\u2019s slope to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n\nHence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(3n\\).  \nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 7,\n\\]\n\nand a direct check shows the inequality also holds for \\(n=3,4,5,6\\).  \nThus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines.  \n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nNow exactly one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non\u2011sunny.  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) can contain points from at most three of the families \\(A,B,C\\) (indeed it can contain at most one point from each, because the three families have distinct slopes).  \nConsequently at least two of the families are **entirely** covered by sunny lines.  \nAs argued above, a sunny line can meet each of those two families in at most one point, so the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of those two families.  \nAdding the at most three points contributed by \\(\\ell\\) gives an upper bound  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+3 = 2n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge 6\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n+1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), the covering is impossible.  \n\nThus no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Complete answer)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \nLemma\u202f3 proves that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.  \nHence the stated set is precisely the collection of admissible values of \\(k\\). \u220e"
