ny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake only lines from the three forbidden families.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal L_{0}&:=\\{\\,y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le \\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\,\\}\\\\\n&\\qquad\\cup\\{\\,x=a\\mid 1\\le a\\le \\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_{0}|=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil=n+1.\n\\]\n\nDelete the line \\(x=\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) if \\(n\\) is odd, or delete the line \\(y=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) if \\(n\\) is even.  \nThe resulting set \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all horizontal or vertical, hence none is sunny.\n\n*Covering proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) unless it was the deleted line (which occurs only when \\(n\\) is even and \\(b=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\)).  \nIn that exceptional case we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present.  \nIf \\(b>\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) then \\(a\\le\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\); the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) unless it was the deleted line (the odd\u2011\\(n\\) case).  \nIf it was deleted we have \\(a=\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) and consequently \\(b\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\), so the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present.  \nThus every point of \\(S\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nChoose a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}:\\; y=m x\\qquad\\text{with }m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n(For concreteness we may take \\(m=\\sqrt2\\); any irrational slope works.)  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{1}:=\\{L_{s}\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_{0