gle non‑sunny line (the vertical line \\(x=2\\)), and it covers the translated set, i.e. the whole of \\(S_n\\) except the first column.  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_n=\\bigl\\{x=1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{+}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly one non‑sunny line (the line \\(x=1\\)).  This completes the induction. ∎  \n\n   Lemma 1 therefore yields a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines (Lemma 2).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Start from the family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of Lemma 1.  Choose any \\(k\\) of its sunny lines and replace each of them by a *dummy* sunny line that does **not** pass through any point of \\(S_n\\).  For a fixed admissible slope \\(\\alpha\\) the set of intercepts \\(c\\) for which the line \\(y=\\alpha x+c\\) meets a point of the finite set \\(S_n\\) is finite; picking any other real number gives a line disjoint from \\(S_n\\).  The dummy lines can be taken with pairwise distinct slopes, so all \\(n\\) lines remain distinct.  \n\n   The unchanged lines still cover every point of \\(S_n\\); the dummy lines are irrelevant because they avoid \\(S_n\\).  Consequently the new family still covers \\(S_n\\) and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the dummy lines are also sunny). ∎  \n\n   Lemma 2 shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny (Lemma 3).**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines cover \\(S_n\\).  Consider the three subsets  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   A&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\n   B&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\n   C&=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All points of \\(A\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), all points of \\(B\\) on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)), and all points of \\(C\\) on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Because a sunny line is **not** parallel to any of these three directions, a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from each of the sets \\(A,B,C\\).  Hence a single sunny line can cover at most three points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   Therefore the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(3n\\).  (The three sets intersect only at \\((1,1)\\); this point can be counted at most once per line, so the bound \\(3n\\) is still valid.)  \n\n   On the other hand  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 7\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n\\) (equivalently \\(n^{2}-5n>0\\)).  Direct verification shows that the inequality also holds for \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) (the left‑hand sides are \\(6,10,15,21\\) whereas the right‑hand sides are \\(9,12,15,18\\)).  Consequently \\(|S_n|>3n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   Hence a covering by only sunny lines is impossible; i.e. the case \\(k=n\\) cannot occur. ∎  \n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 guarantees the existence of a configuration with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\); Lemma 3 excludes the remaining possibility \\(k=n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny** and has one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) such that exactly one of them is non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe construct the family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_0:x=1,\\qquad\nL_1:y=2x-1,\\qquad\nL_2:y=-\\tfrac12x+3 .\n\\]\n\nThe five points of  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nare distributed as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & \\ell_0\\\\\n(1,2) & \\ell_0\\\\\n(2,1) & L_1\\\\\n(2,2) & L_2\\\\\n(3,1) & L_1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines; \\(\\ell_0\\) is the unique non‑sunny line.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}\\) with the required properties exists for some \\(n-1\\ge 3\\).  \nFor \\(S_n\\) keep the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_0:x=1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRemoving the first column gives  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\setminus\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(1\\) from the first coordinate transforms this set into \\(S_{\\,n-1}\\).  \nTranslate every line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}\\) by the vector \\((1,0)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{+}\\).  The translation preserves distinctness of the lines, preserves the fact that exactly one of them is non‑sunny (the vertical line becomes \\(x=2\\)), and makes the family cover the translated set, i.e. the whole of \\(S_n\\) except the first column.\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n=\\bigl\\{x=1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{+}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly one non‑sunny line (the line \\(x=1\\)).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) supplied by Lemma 1; it has exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nChoose any \\(k\\) of those sunny lines.  For each chosen line pick a new line with an admissible slope (different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) whose intercept is chosen so that the line does **not** pass through any point of the finite set \\(S_n\\).  Such a choice is always possible because for a fixed slope the set of forbidden intercepts is finite.  Take the new lines with pairwise distinct slopes, guaranteeing that all \\(n\\) lines remain distinct.\n\nThe lines that were not altered still cover every point of \\(S_n\\); the newly inserted dummy lines avoid \\(S_n\\) and therefore do not affect the covering property.  After the replacement the family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the dummy lines are also sunny). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nNo family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines can cover \\(S_n\\); consequently the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines cover \\(S_n\\).  Define the three subsets of \\(S_n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nB&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nC&=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(A\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), all points of \\(B\\) on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)), and all points of \\(C\\) on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Because a sunny line is **not** parallel to any of these three directions, a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from each of the sets \\(A,B,C\\).  Hence a single sunny line can cover at most three points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTherefore the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(3n\\).\n\nOn the other hand  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n\\); indeed the inequality is equivalent to \\(n^{2}-5n>0\\), which holds for \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) by direct computation and for all larger \\(n\\) because the quadratic is positive for \\(n\\ge 6\\).  Consequently \\(|S_n|>3n\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence a covering by only sunny lines cannot exist, i.e. the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 2 provides, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Lemma 3 shows that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\). ∎"
