+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nIt passes through the two lattice points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\).  \nBecause the equation of \\(S_i\\) can be rewritten as \\(y-1 = x-i\\), for any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) we have the point \\((i,b)\\) on \\(S_i\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\subseteq S_i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe set on the right‑hand side of (3) is exactly the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by the vertical line \\(x=i\\).  \nMoreover, the point \\((i+1,2)\\) belongs to both \\(S_i\\) and the vertical line \\(x=i+1\\); after the replacement it remains covered.  \nHence substituting \\(L_i\\) by \\(S_i\\) does **not** destroy the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining any number \\(k\\le n-1\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) be any subset and put  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(I)=\\bigl\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{L_j\\mid j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{L_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy the argument of §2, each line in \\(\\mathcal L(I)\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) that were covered by the corresponding line in the original family (1).  \nTherefore \\(\\mathcal L(I)\\) also covers \\(T_n\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L(I)\\) is \\(|I|\\).  \nSince \\(|I|\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The case \\(k=n\\)  \n\nFinally replace the non‑sunny line \\(L_n:x+y=n+1\\) by  \n\n\\[\nS_n:\\; y = x-(n-1) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_n\\) has slope \\(1\\) and therefore is sunny.  \nIt contains the points \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n)\\).  \nEach of these points already lies on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) for the appropriate \\(i\\); after the replacement they remain covered.  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{S_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the case \\(k=n\\) is also realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNo other values of \\(k\\) are possible because the number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
