            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering by non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\; y=-x+c .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(D_c\\) is \\(-1\\); hence \\(D_c\\) is **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) and\n\\[\nb = -a + (a+b) = -a + c,\n\\]\nso \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{D_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Sunny lines that replace diagonals  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{c-2}:\\; y = x + (c-2) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{c-2}\\) is \\(+1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nTake any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\). By definition of \\(D_c\\) we have\n\\(b = c-a\\). Substituting this into the equation of \\(S_{c-2}\\) gives\n\\[\nc-a = a + (c-2)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;c = a+b,\n\\]\nwhich is true because \\(c = a+b\\) on \\(D_c\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies the\nequation of \\(S_{c-2}\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(a,b)\\in S_{c-2}\\quad\\text{for every }(a,b)\\in D_c\\cap\\mathbb Z^2 } .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, removing the diagonal \\(D_c\\) and keeping the sunny line\n\\(S_{c-2}\\) does **not** eliminate any point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Families with an arbitrary number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(T\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) be any subset and put  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(T)=\\{S_{c-2}\\mid c\\in T\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n            \\{D_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus T\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  All lines in the first set have slope \\(+1\\), all lines in the\nsecond set have slope \\(-1\\); therefore the two sets are disjoint.\n\n*Cardinality.*  The first set contains \\(|T|\\) lines, the second contains\n\\(n-|T|\\) lines; hence \\(|\\mathcal L(T)|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  Exactly the lines \\(S_{c-2}\\) with \\(c\\in T\\) are\nsunny, so \\(\\mathcal L(T)\\) has \\(|T|\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) let \\(c=a+b\\).  \nIf \\(c\\in T\\) then, by (8), \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{c-2}\\in\\mathcal L(T)\\).  \nIf \\(c\\notin T\\) then the diagonal \\(D_c\\) is retained in \\(\\mathcal L(T)\\), and\n\\((a,b)\\in D_c\\).  Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L(T)\\).\n\nSince \\(|T|\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), the construction yields,\nfor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\nrequired covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Extremal values  \n\n* \\(k=0\\): choose \\(T=\\varnothing\\); we obtain the diagonal family \\(\\{D_c\\}\\).  \n* \\(k=n\\): choose \\(T=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); we obtain the sunny family\n  \\(\\{S_{c-2}\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nEvery such \\(k\\) is realized by the explicit construction \\(\\mathcal L(T)\\) above,\nand no other value of \\(k\\) can occur because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
