one point, because two distinct lines intersect in at most one point. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most one element of \\(P_c\\).\n\n   Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) has only \\(n\\) lines, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by lines different from \\(D_c\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot 1 = n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   However \\(|P_c| = c-1\\ge 2\\) for every \\(c\\ge 3\\). In particular, for each \\(c\\) we need at least two distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) to cover all points of \\(P_c\\) unless one of the lines **is** \\(D_c\\). Because the family \\(\\{P_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) partitions \\(T_n\\) and each part contains at least two points (except \\(c=2\\), which contains the single point \\((1,1)\\)), the only way to cover all parts with exactly \\(n\\) lines is to let each part be covered by a dedicated line, i.e. to take \\(\\ell = D_c\\) for every \\(c\\). Hence every diagonal \\(D_c\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\). \u220e\n\n3. **Consequences for sunny lines.**  \n   All lines \\(D_c\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny (a sunny line must avoid the slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)). By the Lemma, any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) lines necessarily consists exactly of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{D_c\\}\\). Hence the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is forced to be  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The family \\(\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) indeed satisfies the covering requirement, as shown in step\u202f1, and contains no sunny line. Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\nSince no other value of \\(k\\) can occur, the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\; y=-x+c .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Claim 1.* The family \\(\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). Substituting \\(b=c-a\\) into the equation of \\(D_c\\) gives \\(b=-a+c\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some \\(D_c\\). \u220e\n\nEach line \\(D_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.\n\nNow suppose \\(\\mathcal L\\) is any set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\). We prove that \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal D\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\}\\subseteq T_n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|P_c|=c-1\\) and the sets \\(P_c\\) partition \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Lemma.* For each \\(c\\) the line \\(D_c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma.* Assume, to the contrary, that \\(D_c\\notin\\mathcal L\\). Any line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) distinct from \\(D_c\\) meets \\(D_c\\) in at most one point, so it can contain at most one element of \\(P_c\\). Consequently the total number of points of \\(P_c\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most \\(n\\).\n\nIf \\(c\\ge 3\\) then \\(|P_c|=c-1\\ge2\\). Since the families \\(\\{P_c\\}\\) are disjoint, covering all of \\(T_n\\) would require at least \\(\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}1 = n\\) distinct lines, one for each \\(c\\). Because we already have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the only way to achieve a full covering is that for every \\(c\\) there is a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains **all** points of \\(P_c\\). The unique line containing all points of \\(P_c\\) is precisely \\(D_c\\). Hence \\(D_c\\) must be an element of \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradicting the assumption. \u220e\n\nApplying the Lemma for every \\(c\\) yields \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal D\\). Therefore any admissible configuration consists solely of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_c\\), all of which have slope \\(-1\\) and are consequently non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is forced to be\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\nSince the family \\(\\mathcal D\\) indeed satisfies the covering condition, the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\nNo other integer \\(k\\) can occur. Hence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]"

