ach integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\; y=-x+c .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Claim 1.* The family \\(\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). Substituting \\(b=c-a\\) into the equation of \\(D_c\\) gives \\(b=-a+c\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some \\(D_c\\). ∎\n\nEach line \\(D_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.\n\nNow suppose \\(\\mathcal L\\) is any set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\). We prove that \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal D\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\}\\subseteq T_n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|P_c|=c-1\\) and the sets \\(P_c\\) partition \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Lemma.* For each \\(c\\) the line \\(D_c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma.* Assume, to the contrary, that \\(D_c\\notin\\mathcal L\\). Any line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) distinct from \\(D_c\\) meets \\(D_c\\) in at most one point, so it can contain at most one element of \\(P_c\\). Consequently the total number of points of \\(P_c\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most \\(n\\).\n\nIf \\(c\\ge 3\\) then \\(|P_c|=c-1\\ge2\\). Since the families \\(\\{P_c\\}\\) are disjoint, covering all of \\(T_n\\) would require at least \\(\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}1 = n\\) distinct lines, one for each \\(c\\). Because we already have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the only way to achieve a full covering is that for every \\(c\\) there is a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains **all** points of \\(P_c\\). The unique line containing all points of \\(P_c\\) is precisely \\(D_c\\). Hence \\(D_c\\) must be an element of \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradicting the assumption. ∎\n\nApplying the Lemma for every \\(c\\) yields \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal D\\). Therefore any admissible configuration consists