            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;y=-x+c .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of slope \\(-1\\) (hence non‑sunny).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) and  \n\n\\[\nb=-a+(a+b)= -a+c,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\n2\\le c_1<c_2<\\dots <c_k\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_{c_i}=\\{(a,c_i-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,c_i-1\\}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points on the diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\).  \nSelect the first point of each \\(P_{c_i}\\),\n\n\\[\np_i:=(1,c_i-1),\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nand define a sunny line through \\(p_i\\) by\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=2x+(c_i-1-2) .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n(The slope \\(2\\) is rational and different from \\(0,\\pm1\\); any such slope would do.)  \nEach \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the point \\(p_i\\) of the diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\), because two distinct lines intersect in at most one point.\n\nAll points of \\(P_{c_i}\\) except \\(p_i\\) have first coordinate \\(a\\ge2\\).  \nFor every integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V_a\\) contains every point of the form \\((a,b)\\) that lies on any diagonal, in particular it contains all points of the sets \\(P_{c_i}\\setminus\\{p_i\\}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(a\\).\n\nNow define the following family of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}\n   =\\bigl\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\notin\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\bigr\\}\n     \\;\\cup\\; \\{S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\n     \\;\\cup\\; \\{V_a\\mid a\\in A\\},\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(A\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is any set of cardinality \\(k\\).  \nThe three parts are pairwise disjoint because\n\n* the untouched diagonals have slope \\(-1\\);\n* each \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(2\\);\n* each \\(V_a\\) is vertical.\n\nCounting the lines gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_{n,k}|=(n-k)+k+|A|=n .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(c:=a+b\\).\n\n*If \\(c\\notin\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\), and \\(D_c\\) belongs to the first part of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n*If \\(c=c_i\\) for some \\(i\\)*, then either \\(a=1\\) or \\(a\\ge2\\).\n\n*   When \\(a=1\\) we have \\((a,b)=p_i\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n*   When \\(a\\ge2\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\); by construction \\(a\\in A\\), so \\(V_a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sunny‑line count\n\nThe only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_i\\}\\).  \nHence the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
