y \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are sunny, i.e. it contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Why the covering is preserved.*  \n   \u2013 Every point of \\(S_n\\) that does **not** belong to one of the removed rows is still covered by the same line as before (either a remaining horizontal line, or one of the two diagonals).  \n   \u2013 Each point \\(P_{i}\\) lies on the newly inserted sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\).  \n   \u2013 Any other point \\(Q\\) of the removed row \\(y=r_{i}\\) satisfies \\(Q\\neq P_{i}\\).  \n     Such a point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) (because \\(Q\\) has coordinates \\((a,r_{i})\\) with \\(a+r_{i}=n\\) exactly when \\(a=n-r_{i}\\); this value belongs to the row because \\(1\\le a\\le n-r_{i}\\)).  \n     Hence \\(Q\\) is still covered by the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\n   Consequently every element of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Then at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny, i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Any such non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the longest possible row, column or diagonal of the triangular lattice has length \\(n-1\\)).  \n\n   A sunny line has a slope different from the three forbidden slopes; therefore it meets each row, each column and each diagonal in **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is at most \\((n-1)(n-1)\\).  \n\n   Adding the contribution of the possible non\u2011sunny line we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le (n-1)+(n-1)^2 = n(n-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   But by (1)  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n(n-1)\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  \n   Therefore a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the term \\((n-1)\\) in (4) disappears and the inequality becomes even stronger, so the contradiction persists.  \n\n   Hence the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction of \u00a73 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realized, and the counting argument of \u00a74 shows that larger values of \\(k\\) cannot.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x+y=n\\,\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so none is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-1\\)*, then \\(a\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\);  \nif \\(a=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n\\) and again \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nr_{1},r_{2},\\dots ,r_{k}\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nand set  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,\\,r_{i})\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\) and \\(r_{i}\\le n-2\\), we have \\(i+r_{i}\\le n\\); consequently each \\(P_{i}\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\) draw a line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) through \\(P_{i}\\) whose slope is a rational number different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); moreover require the slopes of \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) to be pairwise distinct.  \nSuch a line is sunny, and the distinctness of the slopes guarantees that the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are distinct.\n\nNow replace in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) the horizontal lines \\(y=r_{i}\\) by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{y=r_{1},\\dots ,y=r_{k}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \n\n* Points not lying on any of the removed rows \\(y=r_{i}\\) are covered exactly as before, because the lines that covered them in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are still present.  \n\n* Each point \\(P_{i}\\) lies on the newly inserted sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\).  \n\n* Let \\(Q\\) be a point of a removed row \\(y=r_{i}\\) with \\(Q\\neq P_{i}\\).  \n  Write \\(Q=(a,r_{i})\\). Since \\(a+r_{i}=n\\) is possible for exactly one value of \\(a\\) (namely \\(a=n-r_{i}\\)), the point \\(Q\\) either equals \\(P_{i}\\) or satisfies \\(a+r_{i}=n\\).  \n  In the latter case \\(Q\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x