            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\nd_{x}:\\;y=\\text{const}\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\nd_{y}:\\;x=\\text{const}\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\nd_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}\\quad(\\text{diagonal }-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Row/column/diagonal incidence)\n\n*If a line is parallel to \\(d_{x}\\) (resp. \\(d_{y}\\), \\(d_{d}\\)), then it contains at most one point of each column (resp. each row, each diagonal) of the lattice \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line has a fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate; two points of the same column would have different \\(y\\)-coordinates, impossible. The vertical and diagonal cases are analogous. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Structure of \\(T_{n}\\))\n\n*The set \\(T_{n}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct rows, \\(n-1\\) distinct columns and \\(n-1\\) distinct diagonals \\(a+b=c\\) (with \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\)).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) we obtain the possible rows \\(b=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) and columns \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\), the sum \\(c=a+b\\) can take the values \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\); each such value determines a diagonal intersecting the triangle. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At most two non‑sunny lines)\n\n*In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) there are at most two non‑sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that three of the lines are non‑sunny. By possibly relabelling we may suppose that one is horizontal, one vertical and one diagonal (the three forbidden directions).  \n\nBy Lemma 1 the horizontal line can meet at most one point in each of the \\(n-1\\) columns, the vertical line at most one point in each of the \\(n-1\\) rows, and the diagonal line at most one point in each of the \\(n-1\\) diagonals. Consequently after these three lines there remain at least one column, one row, or one diagonal that contains a point of \\(T_{n}\\) not yet covered.  \n\nSince every uncovered point lies on a distinct row, column or diagonal, an additional line is required for each such uncovered family. Hence at least four distinct lines are needed, contradicting the hypothesis that the whole set consists of only \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nTherefore a covering with \\(n\\) lines cannot contain three non‑sunny lines; at most two may be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence of Lemma 3  \n\nIf \\(k\\) denotes the number of sunny lines, then the number of non‑sunny lines is \\(n-k\\).  \nLemma 3 gives \\(n-k\\le 2\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\in\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = -\\frac{i}{\\,n-i\\,}x + \\frac{n+1}{\\,n-i\\,}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(\\ell_i\\).*  \n\n* The slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{n-i}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n* Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=n+1-i\\); substituting \\(x=n+1-i\\) gives \\(y=i\\). Thus \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((n+1-i,i)\\), both belonging to \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). Set \\(i=\\min\\{a,b\\}\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\)). One checks that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (2) for this \\(i\\); consequently it lies on \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) except those with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2}\\).\n\nThe points with \\(a=1\\) (i.e. \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) and those with \\(b=1\\) are not yet covered. They lie on the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1,\\qquad V:\\; x=1,\\qquad D:\\; x+y=n .\n\\]\n\nAdding these three lines to the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-2}\\) yields exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny. Thus a configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nKeep the horizontal line \\(H:y=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(D:x+y=n\\) (both non‑sunny).  \nReplace the vertical line \\(V:x=1\\) by a sunny line that passes through the same uncovered points. For example, the line\n\n\\[\ns_V:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}x + \\frac{n+1}{\\,n-2\\,}\n\\]\n\ncontains the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\); it is sunny because its slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-2}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\nTogether with the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) defined in (2) we now have \\(n\\) distinct line