the\n   points with a fixed difference \\(b-a=c\\) form such a progression.\n   \u220e\n\n3. **A necessary condition on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points,\n   while a non\u2011sunny line (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n   \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the whole first row,\n   the whole first column, or the whole diagonal \\(x+y=2\\), respectively).  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)\\,n .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   must hold.  A short computation shows that (1) fails precisely when\n   \\(k=n\\); for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) the left\u2011hand side equals\n   \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2+(n-k)\\bigl(n-\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\\ge\n   \\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency: a construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover\n   all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Lines used.*\n\n   * one non\u2011sunny diagonal line  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\;x+y=2;\n     \\]\n\n   * \\(n-1-k\\) vertical non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k);\n     \\]\n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n\n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n     \\]\n\n   All these lines are distinct; the \\(V_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis,\n   \\(D\\) is parallel to the forbidden diagonal, and each \\(S_i\\) has slope\n   \\(1\\neq0,