\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\displaystyle n-1-2\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\le0\\) and it is\n   strictly negative for every \\(n\\ge3\\), the expression is non‑negative\n   exactly when \\(n-k\\ge1\\), i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Hence any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency: a construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover\n   all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Lines used.*\n\n   * one non‑sunny diagonal line  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\;x+y=2;\n     \\]\n\n   * \\(n-1-k\\) vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k);\n     \\]\n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) with *negative* intercepts  \n\n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n     \\]\n\n   All these lines are distinct; the \\(V_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis,\n   \\(D\\) is parallel to the forbidden diagonal, and each \\(S_i\\) has slope\n   \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence the \\(S_i\\) are precisely the sunny lines.\n\n   *Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   **Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-1-k\\).  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   **Case 2.** \\(a> n-1-k\\).  \n   Write \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) (because \\(a\\le n\\)).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+r)=k+1-r .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(b\\ge1\\), (3) forces \\(r\\le k\\) and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   b = (k+1-r)-t\\quad\\text{for some }t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-r\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b = a - i\\qquad\\text{with }i=r+t\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point satisfies the equation \\(y = x - i\\) of the sunny line\n   \\(S_i\\) and is therefore covered.\n\n   **Remaining point.** The point \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(1+1=2\\) and lies on the\n   diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n   \\(\\{D\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\}\\).\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{the diagonal }D)+(n-1-k)\\;(\\text{vertical})+k\\;(\\text{sunny})\n   =n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n   Therefore for the chosen \\(k\\) we have constructed a configuration of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of\n   \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\); the explicit construction\n   shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  \n   Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
