            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (maximal points on a sunny line).**  \n   If \\(\\ell\\) is a sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le m,\\qquad \n   m:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{L1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   *Irrational slope.*  \n   If \\(m\\notin\\mathbb Q\\) then two distinct lattice points of \\(T_n\\) cannot lie on \\(\\ell\\), because the slope between any two lattice points is rational. Hence \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le1\\le m\\).\n\n   *Rational slope.*  \n   Write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \n   For two points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=\\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1),\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(q\\mid(a_2-a_1)\\) and \\(p\\mid(b_2-b_1)\\).  \n   Consequently the set of admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates on \\(\\ell\\) is an arithmetic\n   progression with common difference \\(q\\).\n\n   Because \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the longest possible progression\n   occurs when \\(q=1\\) (the smallest step).  \n   In that case the points on \\(\\ell\\) are of the form  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,\\;pa+c),\\qquad a\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le a\\le n,\\;pa+c\\ge1,\\;a+pa+c\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The condition \\(a+pa+c\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{p+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(p\\neq-1\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)), the right‑hand side is at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\). Hence at most \\(m\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are\n   possible, i.e. \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le m\\). ∎\n\n3. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and exactly\n   \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   *Non‑sunny* lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n   \\(x+y=0\\); each of them can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a whole row,\n   a whole column, or a whole diagonal).  \n   Using Lemma 1 for the sunny lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,m+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (1),\n\n   \\[\n   n^2-k(n-m)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-m)} .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Even \\(n\\).*  \n   Write \\(n=2t\\). Then \\(m=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{2t+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=t\\) and\n   \\(n-m=t\\). Formula (2) gives \\(k\\le\\frac{2t(2t-1)}{2t}=2t-1=n-1\\).\n\n   *Odd \\(n\\).*  \n   Write \\(n=2t+1\\). Then \\(m=t+1\\) and \\(n-m=t\\). Formula (2) yields\n   \\(k\\le\\frac{(2t+1)2t}{2t}=2t+1=n\\).  \n   If \\(k=n\\) all lines would be sunny, and each would have to contain exactly\n   \\(m\\) points (otherwise the total number of covered points would be \\< \\(|T_n|\\)).\n   The only sunny lines attaining the bound \\(m\\) are the lines of slope \\(1\\)\n   with equations \\(y=x-i\\) for integers \\(i\\) satisfying \\(0\\le i\\le n-2\\);\n   there are only \\(n-1\\) such distinct lines inside the triangle.\n   Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible. Consequently also for odd \\(n\\) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus any feasible configuration satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le n-1}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny,\n   covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Lines used.*\n\n   * one non‑sunny diagonal line  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\;x+y=2 ;\n     \\]\n\n   * \\(n-1-k\\) vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k);\n     \\]\n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) with negative intercepts  \n\n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n     \\]\n\n   All these lines are distinct; the \\(V_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis,\n   \\(D\\) is parallel to the forbidden diagonal, and each \\(S_i\\) has slope\n   \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence the \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(a\\le n-1-k\\)**, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   2. **If \\(a> n-1-k\\)**, write \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) (possible\n      because \\(a\\le n\\)).  \n      From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n      \\[\n      b\\le n+1-a = k+1-r .\n      \\tag{4}\n      \\]\n\n      Since \\(b\\ge1\\), (4) implies \\(b = (k+1-r)-t\\) for some integer\n      \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-r\\).  Hence  \n\n      \\[\n      b = a - (r+t) .\n      \\]\n\n      Set \\(i:=r+t\\); then \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and the point satisfies\n      \\(b = a-i\\), i.e. it lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n   3. The point \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(1+1=2\\) and therefore belongs to the\n      diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   The three cases exhaust all possibilities, so every point of \\(T_n\\) is\n   covered.\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{the diagonal }D)+(n-1-k)\\;(\\text{vertical})+k\\;(\\text{sunny})\n   =n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration\n   exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\); the explicit construction\n   shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
