gument would be correct if one additionally required \\(p,q>0\\), but that requirement was never stated).\n\n6. **Choosing minimal \\(a_0+b_0\\).**  \n   The solution picks the point \\((1,1)\\) giving \\(a_0+b_0=2\\). This is indeed the smallest possible sum for points in \\(T_n\\).\n\n7. **Deriving the maximal number of points.**  \n   Substituting the (incorrectly justified) minima \\(|p+q|=2\\) and \\(a_0+b_0=2\\) into (4) yields  \n   \\[\n   M_n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n        =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Because the justification for \\(|p+q|=2\\) is incomplete, the conclusion that **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points** is not rigorously established. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n8. **Attainment example.**  \n   The line \\(y=2x-1\\) (direction \\((1,2)\\)) indeed contains the points \\((1,1),(2,3),\\dots\\) until the sum bound is reached, giving exactly \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points. This shows the bound is *sharp* for the particular direction \\((1,2)\\), but does not compensate for the missing justification of the bound itself.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 – Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n1. **Setup.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\), with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_k\\). This is a correct description.\n\n2. **Capacity argument for sunny lines.**  \n   Using the (unproved) bound from Step 1, each sunny line contains at most \\(M_n\\) points, so the sunny lines together cover at most \\(kM_n\\) points. This follows from the (questionable) bound, so the statement inherits the **Justification Gap** from Step 1.\n\n3. **Claim about non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The solution says: “Since a line parallel to one of the prohibited directions may contain arbitrarily many points of \\(T_n\\), there is **no** numerical restriction coming from those lines; the only restriction is that the sunny lines cannot be forced to cover more points than their capacity \\(M_n\\).”  \n   *Issue:* Later the solution *does* impose a numerical restriction on the non‑sunny lines (see the inequality below). The statement that “there is no numerical restriction” contradicts the subsequent reasoning. Moreover, the later bound assumes each non‑sunny line also contains at most \\(M_n\\) points, which is false. This inconsistency is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n4. **Inequality written.**  \n   The solution writes  \n   \\[\n   kM_n\\ge |T_n|-\\bigl(\\text{points that can be placed on the }n-k\\text{ non‑sunny lines}\\bigr).\n   \\tag{*}\n   \\]\n   This inequality is correct in the sense that the sunny lines must cover at least the points not covered by the non‑sunny lines. However, the right‑hand side is not quantified.\n\n5. **Bounding the right‑hand side by \\(|T_n|-(n-k)M_n\\).**  \n   The solution asserts that the worst case is when each non‑sunny line also contains at most \\(M_n\\) points, giving  \n   \\[\n   kM_n\\ge |T_n|-(n-k)M_n .\n   \\tag{**}\n   \\]\n   *Issue:* This is **not** a worst case; non‑sunny lines can contain *more* than \\(M_n\\) points, which would *reduce* the required contribution of sunny lines, not increase it. Assuming the opposite yields an inequality that is *stronger* than necessary, but the justification for using it as a bound is absent. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n6. **Algebraic manipulation.**  \n   From (**) the solution proceeds:\n   \\[\n   kM_n\\ge |T_n|-(n-k)M_n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   k\\le\\frac{|T_n|}{M_n}- (n-k)\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   *Critical Error:* The first implication is correct, but the subsequent step “\\(k\\le\\frac{|T_n|}{M_n}-(n-k)\\)” is obtained by dividing the inequality \\(kM_n\\ge |T_n|-(n-k)M_n\\) by \\(M_n\\) **and then moving the term \\((n-k)M_n\\) to the right‑hand side**, which is algebraically invalid. Properly,\n   \\[\n   kM_n\\ge |T_n|-(n-k)M_n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;kM_n+(n-k)M_n\\ge |T_n|\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;nM_n\\ge |T_n|.\n   \\]\n   No term involving \\(k\\) remains; the inequality gives a condition on \\(n\\) alone. Consequently the derived bound \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) is **not logically deduced** from the premises. This is a **Critical Error** that invalidates the claimed necessary condition on \\(k\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion of Step 2.**  \n   Because the bound on \\(k\\) rests on the flawed algebra, the statement “any feasible configuration satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\)” is unsupported. The step contains a critical logical mistake.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – Constructions  \n\n#### 3.1  Case \\(k=0\\)  \nThe family of lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=2,\\dots,n+1\\) indeed covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and none of the lines is sunny (they are all parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). This construction is correct.\n\n#### 3.2  Cases \\(1\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\)\n\n1. **Choice of sunny lines.**  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_i: y=2x-(2i-1)\\) are sunny (slope \\(2\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\)). This is fine.\n\n2. **Counting lattice points on each \\(\\ell_i\\).**  \n   The solution derives the maximal admissible \\(t\\) as \\(t_i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2i}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and then defines  \n   \\[\n   m_i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2i}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(i-1).\n   \\]\n   The derivation of \\(m_i\\) is correct given the parametrisation, but the subsequent claim “\\(m_i\\ge M\\) for each \\(i\\)” is asserted without proof. Whether \\(m_i\\ge M=\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) holds for all admissible \\(i\\) and \\(n\\) is not demonstrated; this is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n3. **Total number of points covered by sunny lines.**  \n   The solution states \\(S_k:=\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = kM\\). Since each \\(m_i\\) may be **strictly larger** than \\(M\\), the equality is generally false. No argument is provided to show the sum equals exactly \\(kM\\). This is a **Justification Gap** (and, if taken as a claim, it is false in many instances).\n\n4. **Remaining sums and non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The argument that “the number of distinct sums that appear among the points on the sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\)” is not proved; different \\(\\ell_i\\) could share the same sum \\(a+b\\) for some of their points, reducing the number of distinct sums. Without a rigorous proof, the claim is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n5. **Counting of remaining lines.**  \n   Assuming the previous (unproved) statements, the solution concludes that at most \\(n-k\\) additional non‑sunny lines are needed, matching the available number. Because the premises are unverified, the construction is not rigorously established.\n\nOverall, the construction for \\(k\\ge1\\) contains several gaps and an outright incorrect equality, so it does not constitute a valid proof of realizability.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Conclusion  \n\nThe final statement “\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots,\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\}\\)” relies on the (flawed) upper bound from Step 2 and on the incomplete constructions of Step 3. Consequently the conclusion is not justified.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Errors\n\n| Step | Type | Description |\n|------|------|-------------|\n| 1 | Justification Gap | Claim that \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) without excluding vectors like \\((2,-1)\\). |\n| 1 | Justification Gap | Ignoring lower‑bound constraints when counting admissible \\(t\\). |\n| 2 | Justification Gap | Assuming non‑sunny lines are limited to \\(M_n\\) points. |\n| 2 | Critical Error | Incorrect algebraic manipulation leading to the bound \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\). |\n| 3.2 | Justification Gap | Unproved claim \\(m_i\\ge M\\) for all \\(i\\). |\n| 3.2 | Justification Gap | Incorrect equality \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = kM\\). |\n| 3.2 | Justification Gap | Unproved assertion that the sunny lines produce exactly \\(k\\) distinct sums \\(a+b\\). |\n| 4 |