: horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Impossibility of a single sunny line  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly one sunny line, say \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nConsequently each of those \\(n-1\\) lines belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid \\ell\\parallel\\text{horizontal}\\},\\;\nv=\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid \\ell\\parallel\\text{vertical}\\},\\;\nd=\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid \\ell\\parallel\\text{diagonal }x+y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v+d=n-1\\).  \n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(\\min\\{c,n+1-c\\}\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\); similarly a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(\\min\\{c,n+1-c\\}\\) points, and a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,n+2-c\\}\\) points.  \nSumming these maximal contributions over all \\(h+v+d=n-1\\) lines gives an upper bound\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell_{s}\\}}|\\ell\\cap S_{n}|\n\\le (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand \\(|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell_{s}\\) is sunny, it can meet at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\) (a line of arbitrary slope intersects the integer lattice in at most one point per unit increase of the sum \\(a+b\\)). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\Big|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_{i}\\cap S_{n}\\Big|\n\\le (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor +(n-1)\n<\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}=|S_{n}|\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge 3\\), contradicting the covering requirement.  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for all other \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the two conditions.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake any \\(n\\) distinct lines from the three non\u2011sunny families\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies at least one of the equalities \\(a=c\\), \\(b=c\\) or \\(a+b=c\\) with the appropriate range of \\(c\\); hence it lies on one of the chosen lines. No line is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  The case \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{k}=\\{\\,y=m x\\mid m=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) are pairwise non\u2011parallel and none of them is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); thus they are sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(b\\le k a\\) for some \\(m\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), so the point lies on the line \\(y=m x\\). Consequently the sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose sum of coordinates does not exceed \\(k+1\\).\n\nThe remaining points satisfy \\(k+2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nChoose the following \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Horizontal lines } \\; y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1,\\\\\n&\\text{Diagonal line } \\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach point with \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) either has \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) (hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or else satisfies \\(b\\ge n-k\\); in the latter case \\(a\\le k+1\\) and therefore \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). Thus all points of \\(S_{n}\\) are covered.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nWhen \\(k=n\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(y=mx\\;(m=1,\\dots ,n)\\); the covering condition is automatically satisfied because every point of \\(S_{n}\\) has \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lies on one of those lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe only value of \\(k\\) that cannot be achieved is \\(k=1\\); all other non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
