            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal \\((0,1)\\), vertical \\((1,0)\\) and the diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (no configuration with exactly one sunny line)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\\) of distinct lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_{i}\\supseteq S_{n}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand contains exactly one sunny line, say \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; consequently each of them belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\\mid \\ell\\parallel\\text{horizontal}\\},\n\\quad\nv=\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\\mid \\ell\\parallel\\text{vertical}\\},\n\\quad\nd=\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\\mid \\ell\\parallel x+y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v+d=n-1\\).\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets at most \\(\\min\\{c,n+1-c\\}\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\); the same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,n+2-c\\}\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \nSumming these maximal contributions over the \\(h+v+d=n-1\\) lines gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell_{s}\\}}|\\ell\\cap S_{n}|\n\\le (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe sunny line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) can contain at most one point of each “antidiagonal’’ \\(\\{a+b=m\\}\\); there are at most \\(n-1\\) such antidiagonals intersecting \\(S_{n}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_{s}\\cap S_{n}|\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (6),\n\n\\[\n\\Big|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_{i}\\cap S_{n}\\Big|\n\\le (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor +(n-1)\n<\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}=|S_{n}|\n\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the covering requirement (4).  Therefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (existence for all other \\(k\\))\n\nWe treat three cases.\n\n---\n\n##### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nSelect any \\(n\\) distinct lines from  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\},\n\\;\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\},\n\\;\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies one of the equalities\n\\(b=c\\), \\(a=c\\) or \\(a+b=c\\) with the indicated ranges, so it lies on at least one of the chosen lines.  None of these lines is sunny, hence \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n---\n\n##### (ii)  \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nDefine the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}: y=x+t\\qquad (t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_{t}\\) have slope \\(1\\), which is different from the three forbidden directions; therefore they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\n*Points with \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) and \\(b-a=t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).  Hence every point satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le b-a\\le k-1\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nis already covered by the sunny lines.\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nAssume \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) does **not** satisfy (8); then \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(a+k)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; a\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).  We now add the following \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny vertical lines:\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEach point with \\(b-a\\ge k\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (9).  \n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\ell_{0},\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, has cardinality \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and by the discussion above covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### (iii)  \\(k=n\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}: y=x+t\\qquad (t=0,1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(0\\le b-a\\le n-1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{b-a}\\).  All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(k=n\\) is attained.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1 shows that \\(k=1\\) is impossible.  \nLemma 2 provides explicit constructions for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 1\\).  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find