nWrite a primitive direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) as \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions we have  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) then all lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) are given by (2) above:\n\\((a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nAdding the coordinates yields (3):\n\\((a_0+b_0)+t(p+q)\\le n+1\\).  \nSince (L1) implies \\(p+q\\neq0\\), the integer \\(t\\) can satisfy this inequality for at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\]\n\nvalues, which is at most \\(n\\) because \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) and \\(a_0+b_0\\ge2\\).  \nIf two distinct points of \\(\\ell\\) belonged to the same diagonal \\(D_s\\) they would have the same sum of coordinates, forcing \\(p+q=0\\), contradicting (L1). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) coincides with one diagonal \\(D_s\\) and therefore covers **all** points of that diagonal.  \nHence the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-k\\) whole diagonals.  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(n-k)\n\\]\n\ndiagonals are *not* completely covered by non‑sunny lines; call these the uncovered diagonals.  \nTo maximise the chance of covering the whole set with as few sunny lines as possible we may assume that the uncovered diagonals are the \\(k\\) smallest ones, namely \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\); any other choice would contain a diagonal with at least as many points and would only make the task harder.\n\nThe total number of points that lie in the uncovered diagonals equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{k+1}(s-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{U1}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line can contribute at most one point to each diagonal, therefore on each uncovered diagonal a sunny line can supply at most one point. With \\(k\\) sunny lines the total number of points that can be supplied on the uncovered diagonals is at most \\(k\\) per diagonal, i.e. at most \\(k^{2}\\) points altogether.\n\nThus we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le k^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral,  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, whose union contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \nEach line is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny) and contains precisely the diagonal \\(D_s\\). Their union is \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).*  \n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=2x-(2i-1).\n   \\tag{C1}\n   \\]\n\n   The direction vector \\((1,2)\\) satisfies the conditions of Lemma 1, so each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The point \\((i,i+1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) and belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\). Moreover, because the slope is \\(2\\), no other point of \\(\\ell_i\\) lies on any diagonal \\(D_{j}\\) with \\(j\\le k+1\\). Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover exactly one point from each of the diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\); the set of covered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\tag{C2}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the number of points of \\(T_n\\) already covered equals the left‑hand side of (U1), i.e. \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\).\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are those belonging to the diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \n   For each such diagonal we introduce the line  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=s\\qquad(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\tag{C3}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \n   There are exactly \\((n+1)-(k+2)+1=n-k\\) of them, matching the number of non‑sunny lines we are allowed to use.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 2 shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\).  \nLemma 3 exhibits, for every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor]\\), a concrete configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTherefore the interval gives both a necessary and a sufficient condition, completing the proof. ∎"
