htarrow\\quad k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, whose union contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n**(i) The case \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach of them is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny) and contains precisely the diagonal \\(D_s\\). Their union is \\(T_n\\).\n\n**(ii) The case \\(1\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = 2x-(2i-1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector \\((1,2)\\) satisfies the conditions of Lemma 1, so each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe point \\((i,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=2i-(2i-1)\\), hence \\((i,1)\\in\\ell_i\\). Its coordinate sum is \\(i+1\\); therefore \\((i,1)\\in D_{i+1}\\). By Lemma 1 a line cannot meet the same diagonal twice, so \\(\\ell_i\\) contributes **exactly one** point to the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\). Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover one point of each of the diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) lie in the diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\). For each such diagonal we introduce the line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny. There are  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-(k+2)+1 = n-k\n\\]\n\nof them, exactly the number of non‑sunny lines we are allowed to use.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct (the sunny lines have slope \\(2\\), the non‑sunny lines have slope \\(-1\\)). Exactl