the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n   The two families are disjoint (different slopes), together they give exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and by (1) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013 Lemma\u202f2 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, while Lemma\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable. Therefore the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigsqcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Sunny lines intersect each diagonal in at most one point)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nWrite a primitive direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) as \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions we have  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in\\ell\\) then every lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAdding the coordinates gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+b_0)+t(p+q)=\\text{constant}+t(p+q).\n\\]\n\nSince (4) implies \\(p+q\\neq0\\), for a fixed sum \\(a+b=s\\) equation (5) can be satisfied for **at most one** integer \\(t\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) contains at most one point of each diagonal \\(D_s\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n----------------------------------------------------