) have slope \\(-1\\)), their total number is \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines. This verifies the required coverage for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   Lemma\u202f1 yields the universal bound \\(k\\le n\\); Lemma\u202f2 shows that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable. Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigsqcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) contains at most one point of each diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(\\ell\\) as \\(ax+by=c\\) with real coefficients \\(a,b\\) not both zero.  \nThe three forbidden directions correspond to the cases \\(b=0\\) (horizontal), \\(a=0\\) (vertical) and \\(a+b=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). Hence for a sunny line we have  \n\n\\[\na\\neq0,\\qquad b\\neq0,\\qquad a+b\\neq0 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSuppose \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two distinct lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) belonging to the same diagonal, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nx_1+y_1=x_2+y_2=s .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two instances of the line equation gives  \n\n\\[\na(x_1-x_2)+b(y_1-y_2)=0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_1-x_2=-(y_1-y_2)\\) (the sums are equal), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(a-b)(x_1-x_2)=0 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(x_1=x_2\\) and consequently \\(y_1=y_2\\), contradicting distinctness.  \nIf \\(a=b\\) then (2) forces \\(a+b\\neq0\\), i.e. \\(a\\neq0\\); the line equation becomes \\(a(x+y)=c\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), again contradicting the hypothesis.  \nThus no two distinct lattice points of a sunny line can lie on the same diagonal, proving the claim. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Upper bound)\n\nIf \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\), then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that \\(k\\ge n+1\\).  \nConsider a diagonal \\(D_s\\). By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(D_s\\); therefore the total number of points of \\(D_s\\) that can be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(k\\). Since \\(|D_s|=s-1\\le n\\) for every \\(s\\), when \\(k\\ge n+1\\) the diagonal would have to receive at least one of its points from a non\u2011sunny line. Consequently **every** diagonal would need a non\u2011sunny line, which would require at least \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines in addition to the \\(k\\) sunny ones, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used. Hence \\(k\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Realisation for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). There exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n**Sunny part.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = 2x-(2i-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector \\((1,2)\\) is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\); thus each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) and belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\). By Lemma\u202f1 this is the only point of \\(D_{i+1}\\) lying on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n**Non\u2011sunny part (when \\(k<n\\)).**  \nFor each \\(s\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nm_s:\\; x+y=s .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(m_s\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny. It contains **all** points of the diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) and \\(\\{m_s\\}_{s=k+2}^{n+1}\\) are disjoint because they have different slopes. Their total number is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n+1-(k+2)+1)=k+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nBy construction every diagonal \\(D_s\\) is covered:\n* if \\(s\\le k+1\\) the point \\((s-1,1)\\in D_s\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\,s-1}\\);\n* if \\(s\\ge k+2\\) the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\) lies on \\(m_s\\).\n\nHence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n**The case \\(k=n\\).**  \nWhen \\(k=n\\) we keep only the sunny family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\).  \nFor a fixed diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) the line \\(\\ell_{s-1}\\) supplies the point \\((s-1,1)\\).  \nFor any other index \\(i\\neq s-1\\) solving the system consisting of \\(\\ell_i\\) and the equation \\(x+y=s\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{s+2i-1}{3},\\qquad y=\\frac{2s-2i+1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\) and \\(i\\) have opposite parity, the numerators are multiples of \\(3\\); consequently \\((x,y)\\) is an integer point of \\(D_s\\). Varying \\(i\\) over \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{s-1\\}\\) produces all remaining points of \\(D_s\\). Therefore the \\(n\\) sunny lines together cover every element of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of all possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \