n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigsqcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) contains at most one point of each diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(ax+by=c\\) with real coefficients \\(a,b\\) not both zero.  \nThe three forbidden directions correspond respectively to \\(b=0\\) (horizontal), \\(a=0\\) (vertical) and \\(a+b=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThus for a sunny line we have  \n\n\\[\na\\neq0,\\qquad b\\neq0,\\qquad a+b\\neq0 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two distinct lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) belonging to the same diagonal, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nx_1+y_1=x_2+y_2=s .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two instances of the line equation gives  \n\n\\[\na(x_1-x_2)+b(y_1-y_2)=0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_1-x_2=-(y_1-y_2)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(a-b)(x_1-x_2)=0 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(x_1=x_2\\) and consequently \\(y_1=y_2\\), contradicting distinctness.  \nIf \\(a=b\\) then (2) forces \\(a+b\\neq0\\), i.e. \\(a\\neq0\\); the line equation becomes \\(a(x+y)=c\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), again contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence no two distinct lattice points of a sunny line can lie on the same diagonal, proving the lemma. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = 2x-(2i-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector \\((1,2)\\) is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n#### Non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor every integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nm_s:\\; x+y=s .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(m_s\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non\u2011sunny.\n\nThe families \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) and \\(\\{m_s\\}_{s=k+2}^{n+1}\\) are disjoint (different slopes), and their total cardinality is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n+1-(k+2)+1)=k+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nThus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n\n*Diagonals with index \\(s\\ge k+2\\).*  \nBy definition, the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\) is contained in the line \\(m_s\\); therefore all its points are covered.\n\n*Diagonals with index \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).*  \nFix such an \\(s\\).  Solving the simultaneous equations of \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(x+y=s\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{s+2i-1}{3},\\qquad y=\\frac{2s-2i+1}{3}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\le k+1\\) we have \\(s-1\\le k\\).  For any integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) the numerators in (5) are multiples of \\(3\\): indeed,\n\\[\ns+2i-1\\equiv (s-1)+2i\\equiv 0\\pmod 3,\n\\]\nsince \\(s-1\\) and \\(2i\\) have the same parity, making their sum divisible by \\(3\\).  Hence the pair \\((x,y)\\) given by (5) is an integer point of the diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\nAs \\(i\\) varies, the values of \\(x\\) (and consequently of \\(y\\)) are distinct, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines provide \\(k\\) distinct points on \\(D_s\\).  Because \\(|D_s|=s-1\\le k\\), these \\(k\\) points contain the whole diagonal; explicitly, the points obtained from (5) w