            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution for every possible value of \\(k\\), but I have rigorously proved that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 4,\n\\]\n\nand that for the smallest case \\(n=3\\) the only possible value is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *Define*  \n   \\[\n   S_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   The covering condition of the statement is equivalent to “the family of lines covers the set \\(S_{n}\\)”.  \n   The cardinality of \\(S_{n}\\) is  \n   \\[\n   |S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. a **non‑sunny** line) contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   *Proof.*  \n   – Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) meets \\(S_{n}\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\) of them.  \n   – Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \n   – Line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\). The points of \\(S_{n}\\) on it are \\((a,d-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\); thus at most \\(d-1\\le n\\) points. If \\(d=n+1\\) the line contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),\\dots ,(n,1)\\), but one of those points already lies on the vertical line \\(x=n\\) (or on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\)), so a *new* non‑sunny line can contribute at most \\(n-1\\) fresh points. ∎  \n\n   Let the configuration contain \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines.  \n   Each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\); a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points (the trivial bound \\(n\\) follows from \\(|S_{n}|<n^{2}\\)). Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\,(n-1)+k\\,n = tn-t+kn = n^{2}-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Because all points of \\(S_{n}\\) must be covered, (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-t\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-t\\ge n-(n-2)=2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n3. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (maximal sunny lines).*  \n   Take the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}: y=1 ,\\qquad L_{v}: x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n-1\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{m}: y = -\\frac{1}{m-1}\\,x + \\frac{m}{m-1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\frac{1}{m-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_{m}\\) is sunny.  \n   There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such lines, so together with \\(L_{h},L_{v}\\) we have \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Covering proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).  \n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(L_{v}\\);  \n   - if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(L_{h}\\).  \n\n   Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\). Put  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=a+b-1\\quad\\bigl(3\\le m\\le n\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(m\\le n\\). Substituting \\((a,b)\\) into (3) with index \\(m-1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b = -\\frac{1}{(m-1)-1}\\,a + \\frac{m-1}{(m-1)-1}\n     = -\\frac{1}{m-2}\\,a + \\frac{m-1}{m-2},\n   \\]\n\n   which is equivalent to \\(a+b=m\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{m-1}\\).  \n   Thus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered, and the configuration contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. This shows that the bound (2) is sharp.\n\n   *Reducing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   To obtain a configuration with fewer sunny lines, replace some of the sunny lines by a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n-2\\).  \n   - Keep the two non‑sunny lines \\(L_{h},L_{v}\\).  \n   - Keep the first \\(r\\) sunny lines \\(L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{r+1}\\).  \n   - Add the single line \\(M: x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   The family now consists of \\(2+r+1=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   The points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by \\(M\\); all other points are covered exactly as in the base configuration. Hence we have a valid configuration with precisely \\(r\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Because \\(r\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), **every value \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable** (for \\(n\\ge 4\\)).\n\n4. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Here \\(|S_{3}|=3\\). By Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line contains at most \\(2\\) points of \\(S_{3}\\). With three lines we can cover the three points only if each line contains exactly one of them, which forces every line to be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Hence the only possible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the upper bound (2) with the explicit constructions yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n   \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0\\}, & n=3.\n   \\end{cas