d does not lie on any of the three\nforbidden directions).  \n\nThe line through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and \\(P\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y-1 = \\frac{b_i-1}{a_i-0}\\,x .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a_i\\neq 0\\) and \\(b_i\\neq 1\\), the slope \\(\\dfrac{b_i-1}{a_i}\\) is\nneither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\); moreover \\(b_i-1\\neq -a_i\\) (otherwise\n\\(b_i=1-a_i\\) which would give \\(a_i+b_i=1\\), impossible for \\(i\\ge1\\)).\nHence \\(M_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nNow replace the line \\(L_i\\) in the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) by the sunny line\n\\(M_i\\).  \nThe point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) remains covered (it lies on \\(M_i\\)).  \nEvery other point \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\cap T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=i+1\\) with\n\\(a\\neq a_i\\); consequently its sum is different from the sum of the point\nused for the replacement, so it belongs to a *different* line \\(L_{i'}\\)\n(\\(i'\\neq i\\)) of the original family, and therefore is still covered.\n\nThus the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{L_i\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{M_i\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly **one** sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSelect any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) perform the replacement described in \u00a72, obtaining the\nsunny line \\(M_i\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\Bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{L_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\Bigr)\\cup\\{M_i\\mid i\\in I\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Covering.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=j+1\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\nIf \\(j\\notin I\\) the point lies on the unchanged line \\(L_j\\); if\n\\(j\\in I\\) we chose the replacement point on \\(L_j\\) to be \\((1,j)\\), which\nbelongs to \\(M_j\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(M_i\\) (\\(i\\in I\\)) are sunny, and there are \\(k\\) of them.\nAll remaining lines are the original \\(L_j\\) (\\(j\\notin I\\)), each parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny. Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe lines \\(L_j\\) are pairwise distinct.  \nEach \\(M_i\\) passes through the point \\((0,1)\\) and a different lattice point\n\\((1,i)\\); therefore the slopes \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{1}\\) are distinct, so the\n\\(M_i\\) are pairwise distinct and none coincides with any \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Conclusion  \n\nAll non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nare attainable. No larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because the total\nnumber of lines is \\(n\\). Hence the complete set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
