xis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies (C) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  General construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\qquad\\text{with }0\\le m\\le n .\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(m\\) lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) is covered by the line \\(V_a\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Points still uncovered\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by the lines (2.1) satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m=n-k\\), (2.2) implies  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(R_k\\) is a right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((m+1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(m+1,k)\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(R_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = x-m + t .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(L_t\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently each \\(L_t\\) is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are distinct because they have distinct intercepts.\n\n*Proof that the lines \\(L_t\\) cover \\(R_k\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nt = b-(a-m).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a\\ge m+1\\) we have \\(a-m\\ge 1\\); from \\(b\\le k\\) and \\(a\\le n=m+k\\) we obtain \\(a-m\\le k\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n0\\le t = b-(a-m)\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\) is an integer belonging to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nSubstituting \\(t\\) from (2.5) into the equation of \\(L_t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = x-m + \\bigl(b-(a-m)\\bigr) \\Longrightarrow y = b\\quad\\text{when }x=a .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\) and lies on that line.  \nConsequently every point of \\(R_k\\) belongs to one of the sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Completion of the construction\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = \\{V_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,m\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_t\\mid t=0,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines.  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(L_t\\)’s) are sunny, the remaining \\(m\\) are non‑sunny.  \nBy the discussion in §§2.1–2.3, every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies both conditions (C) and (S).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that fulfills the required covering property and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNo larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
