----------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on\nthe line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies (C) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  General construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\qquad\\text{with }0\\le m\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(m\\) lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points still uncovered  \n\nThe points not covered by the lines (2.1) satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n=m+k\\), (2.2) implies \\(b\\le k\\).  \nConsequently the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(R_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le t\\le\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\ndefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = x + t .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_t\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.\nThe number of integers that satisfy (2.4) is exactly \\(k\\); hence we have\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof that the lines \\(S_t\\) cover \\(R_k\\).*  