 1.  Geometry of \\(T_n\\)\n\nThe three sides of the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nB&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nC&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir cardinalities are \\(|A|=n,\\ |B|=n,\\ |C|=n-1\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|A\\cup B\\cup C|=n+n+(n-1)-1=3n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lattice points in the triangle is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (3) from (4) gives the number of interior points:\n\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Int}(T_n)|\n=|T_n|-|A\\cup B\\cup C|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(3n-2)\n=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering the boundary with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nConsider the three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1\\) is vertical, \\(L_2\\) is horizontal and \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\) lies on one of the lines in (7), so the whole boundary of the triangle is already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering the interior with sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an arbitrary integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nBy (6) the interior contains at most \\(n-2\\) points, hence at most \\(k\\) points.  \nFor each interior point \\((a,b)\\) choose a line  \n\n\\[\nS_{(a,b)}:\\; y=m_{(a,b)}x+b_{(a,b)},\n\\]\n\nwhere the slope \\(m_{(a,b)}\\) is any real number different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) (for instance \\(m_{(a,b)}=1\\)) and the intercept \\(b_{(a,b)}\\) is chosen so that the line passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n