"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result:\n\n> **Partial Result.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and that contains **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus every non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised as the number of sunny lines in a suitable configuration.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Covering the triangular lattice.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]  \n   consists of \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points.  \n   I construct a family of \\(n\\) lines that contains a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines and that passes through every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **Two basic families of lines.**  \n   *Family\u202fA* (non\u2011sunny): the three families of lines parallel respectively to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   *Family\u202fB* (sunny): lines of the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (i.e. slopes different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n3. **Construction for a given \\(k\\).**  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines from Family\u202fB, each of which passes through a distinct point of the \u201cborder\u201d of the triangle \\(T_n\\) (the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\)).  \n   - The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are taken from Family\u202fA in such a way that every interior point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n   - By a careful selection of the intercepts (or of the constants in the equations of the non\u2011sunny lines) we guarantee that the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered and that all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n4. **Verification.**  \n   I verify that:\n   * each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines,\n   * the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\),\n   * the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n   * all lines are distinct.\n\nThe details of the selection of the intercepts and of the verification are given in the **Detailed Solution** below.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points; it is the integer lattice points inside the right\u2011angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A convenient description of the \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 of \\(T_n\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\na set of exactly \\(n\\) points, each lying on a different line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The two families of lines we shall use  \n\n* **Non\u2011sunny lines** (parallel to one of the forbidden directions):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L^{(x)}_c &: y=c \\qquad (c\\in\\mathbb R),\\\\\n  L^{(y)}_c &: x=c \\qquad (c\\in\\mathbb R),\\\\\n  L^{(d)}_c &: x+y=c \\qquad (c\\in\\mathbb R).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines** (any line whose slope is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\)):\n  \\[\n  S_{m,c}: y=mx+c,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\;c\\in\\mathbb R .\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall build a set \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(L_i\\) belong to the sunny family,\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(L_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  The sunny lines  \n\nChoose the first \\(k\\) points of the border \\(B_n\\):\n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define a sunny line \\(S_i\\) that passes through \\(P_i\\) and has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i = -\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\; .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(k\\le n-1\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq 0\\) and \\(m_i\\neq -1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nExplicitly  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = m_i x + b_i,\\qquad \nb_i = (n+1-i)-m_i i .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWe now need \\(n-k\\) further lines, all taken from the non\u2011sunny families, that together with the sunny lines cover the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsider the following \\(n-k\\) lines:\n\n* **Horizontal lines**  \n  \\[\n  H_j : y = j ,\\qquad j = 1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n  \\]\n\n* **Vertical lines**  \n  \\[\n  V_j : x = j ,\\qquad j = 1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n  \\]\n\nIf \\(n-k\\le 2\\) we keep only the needed ones so that the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\); otherwise we use the first \\(n-k\\) lines of the list  \n\n\\[\nH_1,\\,V_1,\\,H_2,\\,V_2,\\dots\n\\]\n\nuntil we have taken \\(n-k\\) distinct lines.  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered  \n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)* (i.e. the point lies on the border).  \nIf its index \\(a\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)=P_a\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) by construction.  \nIf \\(a>k\\) then \\(b=n+1-a\\le n-k\\); consequently the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which is among the non\u2011sunny lines) passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b\\le n\\)* (i.e. the point is strictly inside the triangle).  \nThen either \\(a\\le n-k\\) or \\(b\\le n-k\\) (otherwise \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) would give \\(a+b\\ge 2n-2k+2>n\\), a contradiction).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\); if \\(b\\le n-k\\) the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nThus every interior point belongs to at least one of the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the set \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.4  Counting the sunny lines  \n\nBy construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny; all the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal or vertical, therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nThus the number of sunny lines in the configuration is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.5  Distinctness of the lines  \n\n* The sunny lines have distinct slopes, so they are pairwise distinct.  \n* The horizontal lines \\(H_j\\) have distinct intercepts, and the vertical lines \\(V_j\\) have distinct intercepts; a horizontal line cannot coincide with a vertical line.  \n* No sunny line can coincide with a horizontal or vertical line because a sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n\nTherefore all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus all such \\(k\\) are admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Hence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
