------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{\\ell_{-n+1},\\ell_{-n+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) the integer \\(d=a-b\\) satisfies \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\); by Lemma\u202f1 the point lies on \\(\\ell_d\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore the union of these \\(n\\) lines contains every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n\n1. Choose any \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\) from the interval \\([-n+1,n-1]\\) and consider the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{d_i}\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k)\\).\n\n2. Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S' = S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\ell_{d_i}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of points not yet covered.\n\n3. For each integer \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that \\(S'\\) contains a point whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\), add the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) to the collection.  \n   Because a horizontal line contains **all** points of \\(S\\) having that second coordinate, after adding these lines every point of \\(S'\\) is covered.\n\n4. The number of horizontal lines added is at most \\(n-k\\): at most one horizontal line is needed for each value of \\(c\\), and there are at most \\(n\\) values of \\(c\\); we already used \\(k\\) distinct diagonal lines, so the total number of lines is at most \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n5. If fewer than \\(n\\) lines have been obtained, append arbitrary distinct non\u2011sunny lines (for instance vertical lines \\(x=0, x=1,\\dots\\) that are not already present) until the total number of lines equals exactly \