            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;x-y=d\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(S\\) satisfying \\(a-b=d\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and put \\(d=a-b\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge \\max\\{1,-d\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(b+d)+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2b+d\\le n+1\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;b\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a suitable integer \\(b\\) exists iff the interval  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl[\\max\\{1,-d\\},\\; \\tfrac{n+1-d}{2}\\bigr]\n\\]\n\ncontains an integer, which is equivalent to \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\).  \nConversely, for any integer \\(d\\) in this range, every integer \\(b\\) satisfying (4)–(5) yields a point \\((b+d,b)\\in S\\) lying on \\(\\ell_d\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nAll lines \\(\\ell_d\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(\\ell_d\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{\\ell_{-n+1},\\ell_{-n+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(d=a-b\\) with \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\) by Lemma 1, hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_d\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently the union of the \\(n\\) horizontals contains every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{0},\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1},\n   \\qquad\\text{where }\\ell_{i}:x-y=i .\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 they are sunny, and by Lemma 1 each of them consists of the points of \\(S\\) with \\(a-b=i\\).\n\n2. **Rows that still need a horizontal line.**  \n   Fix a row index \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   The possible values of \\(a-b\\) for points of that row are the consecutive integers  \n\n   \\[\n   1-b,\\,2-b,\\,\\dots ,\\,n+1-2b . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   If the interval (6) contains an integer \\(\\ge k\\) then the row possesses a point whose diagonal is **not** among the chosen \\(k\\) diagonals; such a point will not be covered yet, so we must add the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   The interval (6) fails to contain any integer \\(\\ge k\\) exactly when its largest element satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-2b<k\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;b>\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence rows with  \n\n   \\[\n   b>\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   are already completely covered by the chosen diagonals, while rows with  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   may require a horizontal line.\n\n3. **Number of horizontal lines needed.**  \n   The set of rows that need a horizontal line has cardinality at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n-k . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Add the horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) for all such rows. Up to this point we have at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on either one of the chosen diagonals or on the horizontal line of its row, so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n4. **If fewer than \\(n\\) lines have been obtained.**  \n   When the inequality in (8) is strict, the total number of lines is \\(<n\\).  \n   Append arbitrary distinct non‑sunny lines that are not among the already used horizontals; for instance vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=0,\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots\n   \\]\n\n   chosen so that they are different from each other and from the existing horizontals.  \n   These extra lines are clearly non‑sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and they do not affect the covering property because every point of \\(S\\) is already covered.\n\nThe final collection consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny diagonals and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, and it satisfies the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem demands exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nA line is either sunny or non‑sunny, therefore the number of sunny lines is a non‑negative integer not exceeding the total number of lines, i.e. it must belong to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSince we