other \\(k\\).**  \n   Because we must have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no integer outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\nThe argument above is fully rigorous and addresses the shortcomings pointed out in the bug report.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{d}:\\;x-y=d\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(S\\) satisfying \\(a-b=d\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and put \\(d=a-b\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) we have \\(b\\ge\\max\\{1,-d\\}\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(b+d)+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2b+d\\le n+1\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;b\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus an integer \\(b\\) satisfying both inequalities exists iff  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{1,-d\\}\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\).  \nConversely, for any integer \\(d\\) in this range and any integer  \n\n\\[\nb\\in\\Bigl[\\max\\{1,-d\\},\\;\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Bigr],\n\\]\n\nthe point \\((b+d,b)\\) belongs to \\(S\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_{d}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nAll lines \\(\\ell_{d}\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(\\ell_{d}\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{\\ell_{-n+1},\\ell_{-n+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(d=a-b\\) with \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\) by Lemma 1, hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{d}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor a fixed row index \\(b\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently the union of the \\(n\\) horizontals contains every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n\n1. **Choice of sunny diagonals.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{i}=i-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The numbers \\(d_{0},d_{1},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\) are consecutive integers, centred as much as possible around \\(0\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_{d_{i}}\\mid i=0,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 each line in \\(\\mathcal D\\) is sunny.\n\n2. **Rows that still need a horizontal line.**  \n   Fix a row index \\(b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)).  \n   The set of possible values of \\(a-b\\) for points of that row is the interval  \n\n   \\[\n   I_{b}=\\{\\,1-b,\\,2-b,\\dots ,\\,n+1-2b\\,\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The row is completely covered by the diagonals \\(\\mathcal D\\) iff  \n\n   \\[\n   I_{b}\\subseteq\\{d_{0},d_{1},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since the right‑hand side is an interval of length \\(k-1\\), this inclusion is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   1-b\\ge d_{0}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad n+1-2b\\le d_{k-1}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the explicit expressions of \\(d_{0}\\) and \\(d_{k-1}\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   1-b\\ge -\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   n+1-2b\\le k-1-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Both inequalities are equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a horizontal line is required exactly for the rows  \n\n   \\[\n   b=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Number of required horizontals.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   h:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   An elementary check shows  \n\n   \\[\n   h\\le n-k\\qquad\\text{for all }0\\le k\\le n. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, if \\(k\\) is even, \\(h=\\frac{n+2-k}{2}\\le n-k\\); if \\(k\\) is odd, \\(h=\\frac{n+1-k}{2}+ \\frac12\\le n-k\\).)\n\n4. **Complete family of lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,h\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal H\\) consists of \\(h\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines.  \n   The total number of distinct lines obtained so far is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal D|+|\\mathcal H|=k+h\\le k+(n-k)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   If the inequality is strict, we add any further distinct non‑sunny lines that are not among the horizontals already used (for instance vertical lines \\(x=0,x=1,\\dots\\)). These extra lines do not affect the covering property.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in S\\).  \n\n   *If* \\(a-b\\in\\{d_{0},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny diagonal \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\in\\mathcal D\\).  \n\n   *Otherwise* \\(a-b\\) lies outside that interval; by (7) this can happen only when \\(b\\le h\\), i.e. when the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the selected lines.\n\nThus for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, satisfying the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem demands exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nA line is either sunny or non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines is a non‑negative integer not exceeding the total number of lines. Hence it must belong to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSince we have constructed examples for every integer in this set, no other integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\\).**"
