he admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{d}:\\;x-y=d\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(S\\) satisfying \\(a-b=d\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(a-b=d\\) for some integer \\(d\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(2b+d\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince also \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge -d\\) (because \\(a=b+d\\ge1\\)), the inequalities are solvable precisely when \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\).  \nConversely, for any integer \\(d\\) in this range and any integer  \n\n\\[\nb\\in\\Bigl[\\max\\{1,-d\\},\\;\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Bigr],\n\\]\n\nthe point \\((b+d,b)\\) belongs to \\(S\\) and satisfies \\(x-y=d\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nAll lines \\(\\ell_{d}\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(\\ell_{d}\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{\\ell_{-n+1},\\ell_{-n+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\) covers \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) put \\(d=a-b\\). By Lemma 1 we have \\(-n+1\\le d\\le n-1\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{d}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nd_{i}=i-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1,\n\\]\nand let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_{d_{i}}\\mid i=0,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(d_{0},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\) are consecutive integers, centred as much as possible around \\(0\\); by Lemma 2 every line in \\(\\mathcal D\\) is sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Rows not fully covered by \\(\\mathcal D\\).**  \nFix a row index \\(b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)).  \nFor points of this row the possible values of \\(a-b\\) form the interval  \n\n\\[\nI_{b}=\\{\\,1-b,\\,2-b,\\dots ,\\,n+1-2b\\,\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe row is completely covered by the diagonals \\(\\mathcal D\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nI_{b}\\subseteq\\{d_{0},d_{1},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince the right‑hand side is an interval of length \\(k-1\\), this inclusion is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1-b\\ge d_{0}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad n+1-2b\\le d_{k-1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit expressions of \\(d_{0}\\) and \\(d_{k-1}\\),\n\n\\[\nd_{0}= -\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\nd_{k-1}= k-1-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\ncondition (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a horizontal line is needed exactly for the rows  \n\n\\[\nb=1,2,\\dots ,h,\\qquad h:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Number of required horizontals.**  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\nh\\le n-k. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (5).*  \nIf \\(k\\) is even, write \\(k=2m\\); then \\(h=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-2m}{2}\\bigr\\rceil=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}-m\\bigr\\rceil\\le\\frac{n}{2}-m=n-k\\).  \nIf \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2m+1\\); then \\(h=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-m}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}-m=n-(2m+1)=n-k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n**Final set of lines.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,h\\,\\},\n\\]\nand define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal D\\cup\\mathcal H .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal D\\) contributes \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(\\mathcal H\\) contributes \\(h\\le n-k\\) non‑sunny horizontal lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L|\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal L|<n\\) we adjoin any distinct non‑sunny lines not already present (e.g. vertical lines \\(x=0, x=1,\\dots\\)); this does not affect the covering property.\n\n---\n\n**Coverage verification.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*If* \\(a-b\\in\\{d_{0},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny diagonal \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\in\\mathcal D\\).\n\n*Otherwise* \\(a-b\\) lies outside that interval; by the contrapositive of (3) this can happen only when \\(b\\le h\\), i.e. when the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n---\n\n**Extremal values.**\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(\\mathcal D=\\varnothing\\), \\(h=n\\); we obtain the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).\n\n* \\(k=n\\): formula (4) gives \\(h=0\\); we take the \\(n\\) consecutive diagonals  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{d}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad d=-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n  \\]\n\n  Since every possible difference \\(a-b\\) for \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies in this interval, the \\(n\\) sunny lines alone cover \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem demands exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. Each line is either sunny or non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines is a non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\). Because we have exhibited a construction for every integer in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), no other integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nThe set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
