al D=\\bigl\\{\\ell_{d}\\mid d=d_{0},d_{0}+1,\\dots ,d_{0}+k-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal D\\) consists of \\(k\\) consecutive diagonals, all sunny by Lemma 2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Points not covered by \\(\\mathcal D\\)\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) is uncovered exactly when  \n\n\\[\na-b\\notin[d_{0},d_{0}+k-1].\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFix a column index \\(a\\).  \nThe admissible values of \\(b\\) in this column are  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf (1) holds, then either \\(a-b\\le d_{0}-1\\) or \\(a-b\\ge d_{0}+k\\); using (2) this yields\n\n\\[\nb\\ge a-d_{0}+1\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad b\\le a-d_{0}-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered points in column \\(a\\) is contained in the union of two intervals, each of length at most \\(n-k\\). Consequently **each column contains at most \\(n-k\\) uncovered points**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Covering the uncovered points\n\nFor every column \\(a\\) that contains at least one uncovered point we add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=a .\n\\]\n\nBecause a column can contain uncovered points only when the interval \\([d_{0},d_{0}+k-1]\\) does not intersect the set of differences realised in that column, the number of such columns is at most the number of integers in  \n\n\\[\n\\{-n+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus [d_{0},d_{0}+k-1],\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly \\(n-k\\). Hence we need **at most \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines** to cover all uncovered points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### The final family of lines\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal D\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x=a\\mid a\\text{ is a column that contains uncovered points}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines and at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L|\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal L|<n\\) we may adjoin any further distinct non‑sunny lines (for instance extra horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots\\)); these extra lines do not affect the covering property because every point of \\(S\\) is already covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Verification of the covering property\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\in[d_{0},d_{0}+k-1]\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny diagonal \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\in\\mathcal D\\).\n* Otherwise (1) holds, so by the construction above the column \\(x=a\\) has been equipped with the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Extremal cases\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \\(\\mathcal D=\\varnothing\\).  The uncovered points are all of \\(S\\); we cover them with the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  Here \\(d_{0}= -\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) and the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) consists of the \\(n\\) consecutive diagonals \\(\\ell_{d}\\) with \\(d\\in[d_{0},d_{0}+n-1]\\).  By Lemma 1 these \\(n\\) sunny lines already contain every point of \\(S\\); no non‑sunny line is needed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or non‑sunny; therefore the number of sunny lines must be a non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\).  \nSince we have exhibited a construction for **every** integer in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), no other integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nThe set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act