actly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n   **Proof of Lemma 2.**  \n   - Choose the \\(n-k\\) diagonals  \n     \\[\n     D_{c}= \\{x+y=c\\},\\qquad c=n+2-k,\\;n+3-k,\\dots ,n+1 .\n     \\]\n     These are non‑sunny and are pairwise distinct.  \n   - For each of the remaining \\(k\\) integers  \n     \\[\n     c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1-k,\n     \\]\n     pick **any** two distinct points of the diagonal \\(D_c\\); for instance \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\).  \n     The line through these two points has slope\n     \\[\n     m_c=\\frac{c-2}{c-2}=1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1,\n     \\]\n     hence it is sunny. Denote this line by \\(L_c\\).  \n     Because the two chosen points lie on \\(D_c\\), the line \\(L_c\\) contains **all** points of \\(D_c\\) (a line is determined by any two of its points). Thus \\(L_c\\) covers the whole diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \n\n   - The collection\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L=\\{L_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1-k\\}\\cup\\{D_c\\mid c=n+2-k,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n     \\]\n     consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) lines.  \n     The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_c\\) are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \n     Every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to some diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); that diagonal is either one of the retained non‑sunny diagonals or one of the diagonals replaced by a sunny line, and in both cases the point lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\n   - Distinctness:  \n     * The diagonals kept are distinct because their constants \\(c\\) are distinct.  \n     * The sunny lines \\(L_c\\) are distinct because each is determined by a different pair of points (different values of \\(c\\)).  \n     * No sunny line coincides with a diagonal, because their slopes differ (\\(1\\) versus \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies all requirements and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while Lemma 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le