---------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for every \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can actually be attained.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nFor each \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition the set of diagonals \\(\\{\\ell_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) into two groups.\n\n1. **Retained non‑sunny diagonals.**  \n   Keep the \\(n-k\\) diagonals with the largest constants:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D_{\\text{keep}}=\\{\\ell_c\\mid c=n+2-k,\\;n+3-k,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   These are non‑sunny and distinct.\n\n2. **Diagonals to be replaced by sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(c\\) in the complementary set\n   \\[\n   C_{\\text{rep}}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1-k\\},\n   \\]\n   consider the two points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\) of \\(D_c\\).  \n   The line through them has equation\n   \\[\n   y-1=\\frac{c-2}{c-2}(x- (c-1))\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=x+2-c .\n   \\]\n   Its slope is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the line is sunny. Denote it by\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\; y = x + 2 - c .\n   \\]\n\n   Because the two chosen points lie on \\(\\ell_c\\), the line \\(L_c\\) contains **all** points of \\(D_c\\); indeed any two points of a line determine that line.\n\n   Collect these sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}=\\{L_c\\mid c\\in C_{\\text{rep}}\\}.\n   \\]\n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = \\mathcal D_{\\text{keep}}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}} .\n\\]\n\nCounting gives \\(|\\mathcal L|=(n-k)+k=n\\).  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sun}}\\) consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines, while \\(\\mathcal D_{\\text{keep}}\\) consists of \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c