            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=b\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=a\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(a,b,c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\}\n    =\\{(1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\},\n\\]\nand denote by \\(\\ell_c\\) the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nIn particular  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\,n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\ncontains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\n\nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq-1\\); therefore it meets \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1}\\) in **at most one** point.  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) or a vertical line \\(x=a\\) also meets \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1}\\) in at most one point.  \n\nConsequently, if all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1}\\); but \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1}\\) itself would be missing because no sunny line can coincide with it. Hence at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWe shall exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(i) One diagonal kept.**  \nInclude the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\,n+1}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt covers all points of \\(D_{\\,n+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y = \\frac{n-i}{i}\\,x + 1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{n-i}{i}\\) is a positive rational number; it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so each \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe line \\(s_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,n-i+1),\n\\]\nboth belonging to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\) and \\(1+(n-i+1)=n+2-i\\le n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n**(iii) Horizontal, vertical and remaining diagonals.**  \nAdd the following non‑sunny lines:\n\n* the horizontal line \\(h:y=1\\);\n* the vertical line \\(v:x=1\\);\n* for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+2\\le c\\le n\\) the diagonal \\(\\ell_c:x+y=c\\).\n\n---\n\n**(iv) Counting.**  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\ell_{\\,n+1})\\;+\\;k\\;(s_i)\\;+\\;1\\;(h)\\;+\\;1\\;(v)\\;+\\;(n-(k+2)+1)\\;(\\ell_c)\n= n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**(v) Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.**\n\n*Points on \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1}\\).*  \nAll belong to the kept diagonal \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1}\\).\n\n*Points on a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).*  \nThese diagonals are present in the family, so all their points are covered.\n\n*Points on a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).*  \nWrite \\(c=i+1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \nThe point \\((i,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h\\); the point \\((1,i)\\) lies on the