e slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{y_R-y_{Q_i}}{x_R-x_{Q_i}}\\) that would make the line through \\(Q_i\\) pass through \\(R\\).\n\nSince this forbidden set is finite, a real number \\(m_i\\) not belonging to it exists.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\; y-y_{Q_i}=m_i\\,(x-x_{Q_i}) .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny (its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and meets \\(S_n\\) **only** at the point \\(Q_i\\).  \nChoosing the slopes \\(m_i\\) distinct guarantees that the \\(\\sigma_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThus we have constructed exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering a distinct point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Non‑sunny lines\n\nFor every integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n+1\\) keep the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\; x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll these diagonals have slope \\(-1\\) and are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Counting the lines\n\nThe total number of lines in the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_c\\mid c=k+1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nis  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;(n+1-(k+1))=n .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(\\sigma_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nThe diagonals \\(\\ell_c\\) partition \\(S_n\\) :\n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1} D_c ,\\qquad D_c:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\]\n\n*For \\(c\\ge k+1\\):* the whole diagonal \\(D_c\\) is covered by the line \\(\\ell_c\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*For \\(2\\le c\\le k\\):* the point \\((c,1)\\) belongs to \\(D_c\\) and, by the way we listed the points of \\(S_n\\), we may assume that \\((c,1)=Q_c\\). Consequently \\((c,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\sigma_c\\).  \nAll other points of \\(D_c\