ach diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le k\\) already has **all** its points covered either by a sunny line (the point \\((c,1)\\)) or by the diagonal \\(\\ell_{c}\\) **which we now add**.\n\n     Hence we **add** the diagonals  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_{2},\\ell_{3},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\n     \\]\n\n     to the family. Their number is \\(k-1\\).\n\n   - **Step 5 – counting the lines.**  \n     The total number of lines is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}+\n     \\underbrace{(n-k)}_{\\ell_{k+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}}+\n     \\underbrace{(k-1)}_{\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}}\n     = n .\n     \\]\n\n     (The diagonal \\(\\ell_{k+1}\\) is omitted; the diagonal \\(\\ell_{1}\\) does not exist.)  \n     All lines are distinct: the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(-1\\), while every diagonal has slope \\(-1\\) and distinct intercepts.\n\n   - **Step 6 – verification of coverage.**  \n\n     * Diagonals \\(\\ell_{c}\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) cover all points of \\(D_c\\) except \\((c,1)\\), which is covered by \\(\\sigma_c\\).  \n     * Diagonal \\(\\ell_{k+1}\\) is not kept, but every point of \\(D_{k+1}\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(\\sigma_j\\) that passes through \\((j,k+1-j)\\) (for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\)).  \n     * Diagonals \\(\\ell_{c}\\) with \\(c\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) are kept and therefore cover all points of the corresponding \\(D_c\\).  \n\n     Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n   This completes the proof of the lemma. ∎\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lemma shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists, while the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]"
