thcal L\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n\n   - **Step 1 – choose the diagonal to be replaced.**  \n     Let \\(c_{0}=k+1\\).  \n     The diagonal \\(\\ell_{c_{0}}\\) contains exactly  \n\n     \\[\n     D_{c_{0}}=\\{(1,k),\\,(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1)\\},\n     \\qquad |D_{c_{0}}|=k .\n     \\]\n\n   - **Step 2 – sunny lines covering the points of \\(\\ell_{c_{0}}\\).**  \n     For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) we construct a sunny line \\(\\sigma_{j}\\) that passes through the point  \n\n     \\[\n     Q_{j}:=(j,k+1-j)\\in D_{c_{0}} .\n     \\]\n\n     Fix \\(j\\).  Consider the finite set  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal S_{j}= \\Bigl\\{0,\\infty,-1\\Bigr\\}\n     \\cup\\Bigl\\{\\frac{y_R-y_{Q_j}}{x_R-x_{Q_j}}\n                \\;\\Big|\\;\n                R\\in S_n\\setminus\\{Q_j\\},\n                \\;x_R\\neq x_{Q_j}\\Bigr\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     The first three numbers are the forbidden slopes; the second collection consists of the slopes of all lines joining \\(Q_j\\) to any other point of \\(S_n\\) (vertical lines are represented by \\(\\infty\\)).  \n     Since \\(\\mathcal S_{j}\\) is finite, we can choose a real number \\(m_j\\notin\\mathcal S_{j}\\).  \n     Define  \n\n     \\[\n     \\sigma_{j}:\\; y-y_{Q_j}=m_j\\,(x-x_{Q_j}).\n     \\]\n\n     By construction \\(m_j\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\sigma_{j}\\) is sunny; moreover no other point of \\(S_n\\) lies on \\(\\sigma_{j}\\), because any such point would give a slope belonging to \\(\\mathcal S_{j}\\).  \n     Choose the numbers \\(m_j\\) pairwise distinct; then the \\(\\sigma_j\\) are distinct sunny lines.\n\n   - **Step 3 – the remaining non‑sunny lines.**  \n     Keep all diagonals \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) **except** the single diagonal \\(\\ell_{c_{0}}\\).  \n     The number of kept diagonals is  \n\n     \\[\n     (n+1-2+1)-1 = n-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Each kept diagonal has slope \\(-1\\), hence is non‑sunny, and each of them covers all points of its corresponding set \\(D_c\\).\n\n   - **Step 4 – counting the lines.**  \n     The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of  \n\n     * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_{1},\\dots ,\\sigma_{k}\\);  \n     * the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny diagonals \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\neq c_{0}\\).\n\n     Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     |\\mathcal L| = k + (n-1) = n .\n     \\]\n\n     Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   - **Step 5 – verification of coverage.**  \n\n     * For every \\(c\\neq c_{0}\\) the diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) is present, so all points of \\(D_c\\) are covered.  \n     * For \\(c=c_{0}\\) the diagonal is absent, but each point \\(Q_j\\in D_{c_{0}}\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\sigma_j\\).  \n\n     Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   This completes the proof of the lemma. ∎\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lemma shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions exists.  \n   The trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) (because at most \\(n\\) lines are available) shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
