ny lines while preserving the covering property.  \n   Suppose after \\(t\\) replacements (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(t\\) of which are sunny, and still covering \\(T_n\\). Choose a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\). Let \\(U(L)\\) be the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that are covered **only** by \\(L\\); \\(U(L)\\) is non\u2011empty because otherwise \\(L\\) could be omitted from the original construction. Pick any \\((a_0,b_0)\\in U(L)\\).\n\n   We now select a sunny line \\(S\\) passing through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) but through none of the points already covered by the other \\(n-1\\) lines.  \n   Write \\(S\\) as \\(y=m x + c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) (so \\(S\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction) and choose \\(c\\) so that \\((a_0,b_0)\\) lies on \\(S\\). For each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{(a_0,b_0)\\}\\) already covered by \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\setminus\\{L\\}\\) the condition \u201c\\(S\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\)\u201d determines a single forbidden value of \\(m\\):\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{b-c}{a}.\n   \\]\n   Since there are only finitely many such points, the set of forbidden slopes is finite. Choose a rational \\(m\\) different from all those forbidden values and also different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\). Then the line \\(S\\) is sunny, contains \\((a_0,b_0)\\), and does not meet any other point of \\(T_n\\) already covered.\n\n   Replace \\(L\\) by \\(S\\):\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L^{(t+1)}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\setminus\\{L\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S\\}.\n   \\]\n   The new family still covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the points of \\(U(L)\\) are now covered by \\(S\\)), the lines remain distinct, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one. Repeating this procedure \\(k\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Because the base configuration uses only non\u2011sunny lines, the process can be performed as long as at least two non\u2011sunny lines remain, i.e. for any \\(k\\le n-2\\). Thus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the constructive lower bound gives the complete classification.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points required by the problem: all points with positive integer coordinates whose sum does not exceed \\(n+1\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(slope\\;0),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(slope\\;\\infty),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(slope\\;-1).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound: at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition.  \nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(n,1),\\qquad P_3=(1,1).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(1+n=n+1\\le n+1\\) and \\(1+1=2\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Each of these points can be covered only by a line whose direction is one of the three forbidden ones.*  \nIndeed, if a line through \\((1,n)\\) had a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), then moving one unit to the right would change the \\(y\\)-coordinate by a non\u2011integer amount, contradicting the fact that the line must pass through another lattice point of \\(T_n\\) (the only lattice points on such a line with integer coordinates are those with the same \\(x\\) or the same \\(y\\) or lying on \\(x+y=n+1\\)). A similar argument works for \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,1)\\).\n\nConsequently each of \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) is covered by a line parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\). Since the three directions are distinct, covering three points forces at least two *different* non\u2011sunny lines to appear in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the pigeon\u2011hole principle: with only one non\u2011sunny line all three points would have to lie on that same line, which is impossible because the three points are not collinear).  \n\nHence any admissible configuration contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines, so the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Base configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following families of lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines  \n  \\[\n  H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil).\n  \\]\n* Vertical lines  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor).\n  \\]\n* Possibly one diagonal line  \n  \\[\n  D:\\; x+y=n+1\\quad\\text{(used only when }n\\text{ is odd)}.\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\lceil n/2\\rceil+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nn &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2pt]\nn-1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd we add the diagonal line \\(D\\) to obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n*If \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).*  \n*If \\(a>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), so the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).*  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.  \nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Turning non\u2011sunny lines into sunny ones\n\nWe now show how to increase the number of sunny lines while preserving the covering property, up to the limit \\(n-2\\).\n\n#### Lemma 4.1 (single replacement)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing at least one non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that replacing \\(L\\) by \\(S\\) yields another family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nU(L)=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid (a,b)\\text{ lies on }L\\text{ and on no other line of }\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(U(L)\\neq\\varnothing\\); otherwise \\(L\\) would be redundant and could have been omitted from the original construction, contradicting minimality of the base configuration.\n\nChoose \\((a_0,b_0)\\in U(L)\\).  \nWrite a sunny line through this point as \\(y=m x + c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) (the slope \\(\\infty\\) corresponds to a vertical line, also forbidden).  \nSet \\(c=b_0-m a_0\\) so that the line passes through \\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\nFor each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{(a_0,b_0)\\}\\) that is already covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\) the condition \u201c\\(y=m x + c\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\)\u201d forces  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b-c}{a}.\n\\]\n\nThus each such point forbids at most one rational value of \\(m\\). Since the set of already\u2011covered points is finite, the collection of forbidden slopes is finite. Choose a rational \\(m\\) different from all forbidden values and also different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\). The resulting line \\(S\\) is sunny, contains \\((a_0,b_0)\\), and does not pass through any other point of \\(T_n\\) that is already covered.\n\nNow replace \\(L\\) by \\(S\\).  \nAll points of \\(U(L)\\) are now covered by \\(S\\); all other points remain covered by the unchanged lines.  \nThe new family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Inductive construction\n\nStart from the base configuration of Section\u202f3, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines and \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nApply Lemma\u202f4.1 repeatedly. After the \\(t\\)-th application (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still covering \\(T_n\\).  \n\nBecause \\(t\\le k-1\\le n-3\\), at least two non\u2011sunny lines remain, so Lemma\u202f4.1 can be applied again.  \nAfter \\(k\\) steps we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering \\(T_n\\).\n\nSince the process works for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), every such \\(k\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
