  \\mathcal{F}_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{L_{k+2},L_{k+3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n   It consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to a sum\u2011line \\(L_{a+b}\\) (if \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\)) or to one of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (if \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), because then \\(a\\le k\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\((a,1)\\) of slope \\(m_a\\)). Hence the covering condition holds.  \n   By construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny, so the family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); it contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessity of \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nA family of lines satisfying the problem statement contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Clearly \\(k\\) cannot be negative.  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \n\n#### 2.1.  The non\u2011sunny \u201csum\u2011lines\u201d\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\) lie on \\(L_s\\).  The line \\(L_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y