"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two independent parts.\n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We show that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least two lines whose direction is one of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   The argument uses the three \u201ccorner\u2019\u2019 points of the required set  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{h}=(1,n),\\qquad P_{v}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{d}=(1,1)\n   \\]\n\n   and the fact that a line of sunny direction can contain **at most one** of these three points.  \n   Consequently at least two of the three points are covered by non\u2011sunny lines, which forces the presence of at least two distinct non\u2011sunny lines. Hence at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny.\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (no sunny lines).*  \n   For any \\(n\\ge3\\) we exhibit an explicit family \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, each parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, that covers all points  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The construction uses \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), and, when \\(n\\) is odd, the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). A short verification shows that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Turning non\u2011sunny lines into sunny ones.*  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) we replace non\u2011sunny lines one by one by sunny lines while preserving the covering property.  \n\n   **Lemma 4.1 (single replacement).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\). Then there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma 4.1.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   U(L)=\\{P\\in T_n\\mid P\\text{ lies on }L\\text{ and on no other line of }\\mathcal L\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\mathcal L\\) has exactly \\(n\\) lines and \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\) points, the family cannot contain a redundant line; otherwise we could delete that line and still cover all points, contradicting the minimality of the construction of \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) (which uses each line essentially). Hence \\(U(L)\\neq\\varnothing\\). Choose a point \\(P_0=(a_0,b_0)\\in U(L)\\).\n\n   Write a sunny line through \\(P_0\\) as \\(y=m x +c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\) satisfying \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) (the case of a vertical line is excluded because it would be non\u2011sunny). Set \\(c=b_0-m a_0\\) so that the line passes through \\(P_0\\).\n\n   For any other point \\(Q=(a,b)\\in T_n\\) that is already covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\), the condition \u201c\\(Q\\) lies on the new line\u2019\u2019 forces a single value of the slope:\n\n   \\[\n   m_Q=\\frac{b-c}{a}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus each such point forbids at most one rational value of \\(m\\). Since the set of already\u2011covered points is finite, the collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M=\\{0,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_Q\\mid Q\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P_0\\},\\;Q\\text{ covered by }\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Choose a rational number \\(m\\notin\\mathcal M\\); this is possible because \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite. With this choice the line \\(S:y=m x +c\\) is sunny, contains \\(P_0\\), does **not** pass through any other point already covered by the other \\(n-1\\) lines, and is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise it would share the same slope and intercept with an existing line, contradicting the exclusion of the corresponding slope). Consequently \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n   **Inductive construction.**  \n   Begin with \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}=\\mathcal B_n\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines).  \n   Suppose after \\(t\\) steps (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines. Because \\(t\\le k-1\\le n-3\\), at least two non\u2011sunny lines remain in \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\); pick any one of them and apply Lemma\u202f4.1 to obtain \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) with \\(t+1\\) sunny lines. Repeating this procedure \\(k\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The process works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) because the base configuration contains \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines, so we can perform at most \\(n-2\\) replacements before we would be forced to keep at least two non\u2011sunny lines (required by the upper\u2011bound argument). Hence every such \\(k\\) is attainable.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the constructive lower bound completes the classification.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is *not* horizontal (\\(slope=0\\)), vertical (\\(slope=\\infty\\)) nor slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_h=(1,n),\\qquad P_v=(n,1),\\qquad P_d=(1,1),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(T_n\\).\n\n> **Claim.** A sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points \\(\\{P_h,P_v,P_d\\}\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains two of them, say \\(P_h\\) and \\(P_v\\). Then the slope of \\(L\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{\\,n-1\\,}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden; hence such a line cannot be sunny.  \nIf \\(L\\) contained \\(P_h\\) and \\(P_d\\), its slope would be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{\\,1-1\\,}=\\infty,\n\\]\n\ni.e. vertical, also forbidden.  \nSimilarly a line through \\(P_v\\) and \\(P_d\\) would be horizontal. \u220e\n\nThus each of the three points must be covered by a line whose direction is one of the three forbidden directions. Because the three directions are distinct, at least two *different* non\u2011sunny lines are needed to cover the three points (pigeon\u2011hole principle). Consequently any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines, and therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Base configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following families of lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines  \n  \\[\n  H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil).\n  \\]\n\n* Vertical lines  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor).\n  \\]\n\n* Diagonal line (used only when \\(n\\) is odd)  \n  \\[\n  D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\lceil n/2\\rceil+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nn & (n\\text{ even}),\\\\[2pt]\nn-1 & (n\\text{ odd}),\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nand when \\(n\\) is odd we add the diagonal line \\(D\\) to obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n**Coverage verification.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n*If \\(a>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), so the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one\n\n#### Lemma 4.1  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\). Then there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\\) also covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU(L)=\\{P\\in T_n\\mid P\\text{ lies on }L\\text{ and on no other line of }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(U(L)=\\varnothing\\) then \\(L\\) is redundant: removing it would leave all points still covered, contradicting the construction of the base family \\(\\mathcal B_n\\), in which every line is essential (each horizontal line \\(y=i\\) is the only line covering the points with \\(b=i\\) and \\(a>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\); similarly for each vertical line). Hence \\(U(L)\\neq\\varnothing\\). Choose \\(P_0=(a_0,b_0)\\in U(L)\\).\n\nWrite a line through \\(P_0\\) as \\(y=m x +c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\). Set \\(c=b_0-m a_0\\). We shall choose \\(m\\) so that the line is sunny and does not pass through any point already covered by the other \\(n-1\\) lines.\n\nFor each point \\(Q=(a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P_0\\}\\) that is covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\), the condition \u201c\\(Q\\) lies on the new line\u2019\u2019 forces the slope  \n\n\\[\nm_Q=\\frac{b-c}{a}.\n\\]\n\nThus each such point forbids at most one rational value of \\(m\\). Collect all forbidden slopes together with the two forbidden slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{0,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_Q\\mid Q\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P_0\\},\\;Q\\text{ covered by }\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal F\\) is a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Choose a rational number \\(m\\notin\\mathcal F\\); this is possible because \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite. With this choice the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y=m x +c\n\\]\n\nhas slope different from \\(0,-1\\) (hence is sunny), passes through \\(P_0\\), and does **not** pass through any other point already covered by the remaining lines. Moreover \\(S\\) cannot coincide with any existing line, because coincidence would require both the same slope and the same intercept, which is excluded by the construction of \\(m\\). Consequently \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Inductive construction of configurations with any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nStart from the base family \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) (Section\u202f3), which contains \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and no sunny lines.\n\nAssume we have already performed \\(t\\) replacements (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) and obtained a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines. Because the original family had \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines, after \\(t\\) replacements there remain \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Since \\(t\\le k-1\\le n-3\\), we have \\(n-t\\ge 3\\); in particular at least one non\u2011sunny line is still present. Choose any such line \\(L\\) and apply Lemma\u202f4.1 to replace it by a sunny line. The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\) and now has \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nRepeating this step \\(k\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. The process stops when only two non\u2011sunny lines remain, which is precisely the situation forced by the upper bound; therefore the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, giving the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\);\n* for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"

