n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   A direct check shows that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal B_n\\). All lines of \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Lemma (sunny line through a prescribed point).**  \n   Let \\(S\\subseteq T_n\\) be a finite set of lattice points and let \\(P\\in S\\).  \n   There exists a sunny line \\(L\\) passing through \\(P\\) that does **not** contain any other point of \\(S\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  Write a line through \\(P=(a_0,b_0)\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\) and intercept \\(c=b_0-ma_0\\).  \n   For each other point \\(Q=(a,b)\\in S\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) the condition “\\(Q\\) lies on the line’’ forces the unique slope \\(m_Q=(b-c)/a\\).  \n   The set \\(\\{0,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_Q\\mid Q\\in S\\setminus\\{P\\}\\}\\) is finite; choose a rational \\(m\\) outside it (possible because \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite).  \n   The resulting line has slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) (hence is sunny) and contains no point of \\(S\\) other than \\(P\\). ∎\n\n4. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – choose \\(k\\) “special’’ points.*  \n   Select distinct points  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n   They all belong to \\(T_n\\) and lie on different rows and columns.\n\n   *Step 2 – build sunny lines.*  \n   Apply the Lemma to the set \\(S_k=\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\\) repeatedly: for each \\(P_i\\) obtain a sunny line \\(L_i\\) that passes through \\(P_i\\) and avoids all other points of \\(S_k\\). Because the slopes are chosen from a set avoiding finitely many rational numbers, the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct and sunny.\n\n   *Step 3 – cover the remaining points.*  \n   Remove from \\(T_n\\) the \\(k\\) special points; denote the remainder by \\(T_n' = T_n\\setminus S_k\\).  \n   Observe that every point of \\(T_n'\\) has either its \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) or its \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) (indeed, if both coordinates were \\(>n-k\\) then their sum would exceed \\(n+1\\)).  \n   Consequently the following family of non‑sunny lines covers \\(T_n'\\):\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{n,k}= \\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\,\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal V_{n,k}= \\{\\,x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   (When \\(n-k\\) is odd we may also add the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\); this line is non‑sunny and does not interfere with the count.)\n\n   The total number of lines used is\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal H_{n,k}|+|\\mathcal V_{n,k}|+k\n   = (n-k-1)+(n-k-1)+k = n .\n   \\]\n   Thus we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n5. **Putting the two parts together.**  \n   The upper‑bound argument shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For all }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is possible }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
