re exists a sunny line passing through \\(P\\) that contains no other point of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write a line through \\(P=(a_{0},b_{0})\\) as \\(y=m x +c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\) and intercept \\(c=b_{0}-m a_{0}\\).  \n   For each \\(Q=(a,b)\\in S\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) the condition \u201c\\(Q\\) lies on the line\u2019\u2019 forces the unique slope \\(m_{Q}=(b-c)/a\\).  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M:=\\{0,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_{Q}\\mid Q\\in S\\setminus\\{P\\}\\}\n   \\]\n   is a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\).  \n   Choose a rational number \\(m\\notin\\mathcal M\\) (possible because \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite).  \n   The line \\(y=m x +c\\) has slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) (hence is sunny) and, by construction, contains no point of \\(S\\) other than \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 special diagonal points.*  \n   For the given \\(k\\) choose the distinct points  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{i}=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   All \\(D_{i}\\) belong to \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 sunny lines through the special points.*  \n   Apply the Lemma to the finite set \\(S_{k}=\\{D_{1},\\dots ,D_{k}\\}\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) we obtain a sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) that passes through \\(D_{i}\\) and avoids all other points of \\(S_{k}\\).  \n   Because the slopes are chosen outside a finite set, the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are pairwise distinct and sunny.\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 non\u2011sunny covering of the remaining points.*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_{n}=T_{n}\\setminus S_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T'_{n}\\) we have either \\(b\\le n-k\\) or \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n   Indeed, if both \\(a>n-k\\) and \\(b>n-k\\) then  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b>2(n-k)\\ge n+2,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(k\\le n-2\\) implies \\(2(n-k)\\ge n+2\\); this contradicts the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T'_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the following families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{n,k}= \\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\,\\},\n   \\qquad\n   \\mathcal V_{n,k}= \\{\\,x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   - If \\(b\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H_{n,k}\\).  \n   - If \\(b>n-k\\) then necessarily \\(a\\le n-k\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V_{n,k}\\).\n\n   Thus \\(\\mathcal H_{n,k}\\cup\\mathcal V_{n,k}\\) covers all points of \\(T'_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 reducing the number of non\u2011sunny lines to \\(n-k\\).*  \n   The union \\(\\mathcal H_{n,k}\\cup\\mathcal V_{n,k}\\) contains \\(2(n-k)\\) lines, which is too many because together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines we would have \\(2n-k\\) lines.  \n   Observe that the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (slope \\(-1\\)) is non\u2011sunny and passes through **all** points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) with \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   Replace, for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\), the vertical line \\(x=j\\) by the single line \\(D\\).  \n   After this replacement we keep:\n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\);\n   * the horizontal lines \\(\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\}\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) of them);\n   * the vertical lines \\(\\{x=j\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n-k\\}\\) (there are \\(n-2k\\) of them, possibly zero);\n   * the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   Counting, the total number of non\u2011sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k) \\;+\\; (n-2k) \\;+\\; 1 \\;=\\; n-k,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\((n-2k)+1 = k\\) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines have already been counted separately.  \n   All these lines are distinct: the horizontal and vertical lines are axis\u2011parallel, the diagonal line has slope \\(-1\\), and the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0,-1\\) and are not vertical by construction.\n\n   *Step\u202f5 \u2013 final 